rect.\n\nSo, the sides of triangle ACD are AC = 3\u221a3 + 3, CD = 3 + \u221a3, and DA = 6 + 2\u221a3.\n\nNow, let's check if these lengths make sense. In a regular hexagon, the distance between two vertices separated by one other vertex (like A and C) is equal to the side length of the hexagon multiplied by sqrt(3). Wait, actually, let me recall: in a regular hexagon with side length 's', the distance between opposite vertices (diameter) is 2s. The distance between two vertices with one vertex in between is 2s sin(60\u00b0) = s*sqrt(3). But in our case, the radius is given as 3 + sqrt(3). Wait, perhaps I need to confirm the side length of the hexagon.\n\nIn a regular hexagon, the radius (circumradius) is equal to the side length. Because in a regular hexagon, each side length is equal to the radius of the circumscribed circle. Wait, is that right?\n\nYes, actually in a regular hexagon, the side length is equal to the radius of the circumscribed circle. So, if the radius is R, then each side of the hexagon is R. So, in our case, the radius is \\(3 + \\sqrt{3}\\), so each side of the hexagon is \\(3 + \\sqrt{3}\\). Therefore, the length from A to B is \\(3 + \\sqrt{3}\\). But then, the distance between A and C, which is two sides apart, would be 2 * R * sin(60\u00b0) as calculated before. Let me verify.\n\nWait, perhaps I confused chord length with the side length. Wait, in a regular hexagon, the side length is equal to the radius. So, if the radius is R, then the chord length between adjacent vertices is R. Wait, chord length formula is 2R sin(theta/2). For adjacent vertices, theta is 60 degrees, so chord length is 2R sin(30\u00b0) = 2R*(1/2) = R. Therefore, in a regular hexagon, the side length is R, which is equal to the radius. So in this problem, the radius is given as \\(3 + \\sqrt{3}\\), so the side length of the hexagon is \\(3 + \\sqrt{3}\\). Therefore, sides AB, BC, CD, etc., are all \\(3 + \\sqrt{3}\\).\n\nBut then, the distance between A and C (two vertic