o:\n\nsqrt[(x - 4)^2 + y^2] / |x - 3| = 2\n\nNow, to eliminate the ratio, I can cross-multiply:\n\nsqrt[(x - 4)^2 + y^2] = 2 |x - 3|\n\nTo remove the square root, I can square both sides. But before that, I should consider whether squaring both sides will introduce extraneous solutions. However, since we're dealing with distances, which are non-negative, squaring should be okay here. Let's proceed.\n\nSquaring both sides:\n\n[(x - 4)^2 + y^2] = [2 |x - 3|]^2\n\nBut since the square of an absolute value is the square of the expression inside, we can write:\n\n(x - 4)^2 + y^2 = 4(x - 3)^2\n\nNow, let's expand both sides.\n\nLeft side: (x - 4)^2 + y^2\n= (x^2 - 8x + 16) + y^2\n\nRight side: 4(x - 3)^2\n= 4(x^2 - 6x + 9)\n= 4x^2 - 24x + 36\n\nSo, putting it all together:\n\nx^2 - 8x + 16 + y^2 = 4x^2 - 24x + 36\n\nNow, let's bring all terms to one side to simplify:\n\nLeft side minus right side:\n\nx^2 - 8x + 16 + y^2 - 4x^2 + 24x - 36 = 0\n\nCombine like terms:\n\n(1x^2 - 4x^2) + (-8x + 24x) + (16 - 36) + y^2 = 0\n\nWhich is:\n\n-3x^2 + 16x - 20 + y^2 = 0\n\nMultiply through by -1 to make the coefficients more positive:\n\n3x^2 - 16x + 20 - y^2 = 0\n\nRearranged:\n\n3x^2 - y^2 - 16x + 20 = 0\n\nLooking at the options, option C is exactly this: 3x\u00b2 - y\u00b2 -16x +20=0. So that's one of the choices. But let me verify if I made any mistakes.\n\nWait, let me check the steps again. Starting from the ratio:\n\nsqrt[(x-4)^2 + y^2] / |x - 3| = 2\n\nCross multiply:\n\nsqrt[(x-4)^2 + y^2] = 2|x - 3|\n\nSquare both sides:\n\n(x-4)^2 + y^2 = 4(x - 3)^2\n\nExpand left side: x\u00b2 -8x +16 + y\u00b2\n\nRight side: 4x\u00b2 -24x +36\n\nBring all to left:\n\nx\u00b2 -8x +16 + y\u00b2 -4x\u00b2 +24x -36 =0\n\nCombine:\n\n(1 -4)x\u00b2 + (-8 +24)x + (16 -36) + y\u00b2 =0\n\nWhich is: -3x\u00b2 +16x -20 + y\u00b2=0\n\nMultiply by -1: 3x\u00b2 -16x +20 - y\u00b2=0 => 3x\u00b2 - y\u00b2 -16x +20=0. Yes, that's correct. So option C.\n\nBut let's check the other options. Option A and 