ait, the divisors of 77 are only 1,7,11,77. So we considered all possible d. Hence, the solutions are n=4,99,175,1444.\n\nBut let me check if there could be other solutions. Suppose there is another value of d. Wait, d must divide 77, so only those four. Since d=77 gives no solution, and the others give the solutions as above. Therefore, these are all solutions.\n\nBut let me think again. Maybe there's another way to approach the problem. Let's consider the equation n\u00b2 +77n =k\u00b2. Rearranged as k\u00b2 -n\u00b2=77n, which factors as (k -n)(k +n)=77n. Now, since k >n (because k\u00b2 =n\u00b2 +77n >n\u00b2), both (k -n) and (k +n) are positive integers. Let me denote s =k -n and t =k +n, so s*t=77n. Also, s and t have the same parity because k -n +k +n=2k is even, so s and t must both be even or both be odd. But since their product is 77n, which could be even or odd depending on n. Wait, n is a natural number, so if n is even, 77n is even; if n is odd, 77n is odd. Therefore, s and t must both be even if n is even, and both odd if n is odd.\n\nMoreover, since s and t satisfy t -s =2n. Because t =k +n and s =k -n, so t -s=2n.\n\nSo we have s*t=77n and t -s=2n. Let me write t =s +2n. Then substituting into the first equation:\n\ns*(s +2n)=77n\n\nExpand this:\n\ns\u00b2 +2n*s =77n\n\nRearranged as:\n\ns\u00b2 +2n*s -77n=0\n\nThis is a quadratic equation in terms of s. Let's solve for s:\n\ns = [-2n \u00b1 sqrt(4n\u00b2 + 4*77n)] /2\n\nSimplify:\n\ns = [-2n \u00b1 sqrt(4n\u00b2 +308n)] /2\n\n= [-2n \u00b1 2*sqrt(n\u00b2 +77n)] /2\n\n= -n \u00b1 sqrt(n\u00b2 +77n)\n\nBut s must be positive, so we take the positive root:\n\ns = -n + sqrt(n\u00b2 +77n)\n\nBut since s must be an integer, sqrt(n\u00b2 +77n) must be an integer, which is k. So this brings us back to the original equation. Hmm, not sure if this helps. Maybe another approach.\n\nAlternatively, from s*t=77n and t -s=2n. Let's solve for t and s in terms of n. Let me denote t =s +2n. Then substituting into s*