 valid solutions as it leads to non-natural numbers.\n\nVerifying each solution confirms \\( n = 4, 99, 175, 1444 \\) are correct. Thus, the natural numbers \\( n \\) are:\n\n\\[\n\\boxed{4}, \\boxed{99}, \\boxed{175}, \\boxed{1444}\n\\]", "ground_truth_solution": "\n1. We need to find all natural numbers \\( n \\) such that \\( n^2 + 77n \\) is a perfect square of a natural number. This can be written as:\n   \\[\n   n(n + 77) = m^2\n   \\]\n   where \\( m \\) and \\( n \\) are natural numbers.\n\n2. We will consider the following cases based on the value of \\(\\gcd(n, 77)\\):\n\n#### Case 1: \\(\\gcd(n, 77) = 1\\)\n\n- In this case, the numbers \\( n \\) and \\( n + 77 \\) are coprime. Hence, each of them must be a perfect square:\n  \\[\n  n = p^2 \\quad \\text{and} \\quad n + 77 = q^2\n  \\]\n- This implies:\n  \\[\n  q^2 - p^2 = 77\n  \\]\n  Using the difference of squares factorization:\n  \\[\n  (q - p)(q + p) = 77\n  \\]\n  The factors of 77 are \\((1, 77)\\) and \\((7, 11)\\):\n\n  - For \\((q - p, q + p) = (1, 77)\\):\n    \\[\n    q - p = 1 \\quad \\text{and} \\quad q + p = 77\n    \\]\n    Adding these equations:\n    \\[\n    2q = 78 \\implies q = 39\n    \\]\n    Hence:\n    \\[\n    p = 38 \\implies n = p^2 = 38^2 = 1444\n    \\]\n\n  - For \\((q - p, q + p) = (7, 11)\\):\n    \\[\n    q - p = 7 \\quad \\text{and} \\quad q + p = 11\n    \\]\n    Adding these equations:\n    \\[\n    2q = 18 \\implies q = 9\n    \\]\n    Hence:\n    \\[\n    p = 2 \\implies n = p^2 = 2^2 = 4\n    \\]\n\n#### Case 2: \\(\\gcd(n, 77) = 7\\)\n\n- Let \\( n = 7s \\), where \\( s \\) is not divisible by 11. Then:\n  \\[\n  49s(s + 11) = m^2\n  \\]\n  Since \\( s \\) and \\( s + 11 \\) are coprime, both need to be perfect squares:\n  \\[\n  s = p^2 \\quad \\text{and} \\quad s + 11 = q^2\n  \\]\n  This implies:\n  \\[\n  q^2 - p^2 = 11\n  \\]\n  Using the difference of squares factorization:\n  \\[\n  (q - p)(q + p) = 11\n  \\]\n  The possible pair is \\((1, 11)\\):\n  \\[\n  q - p = 1 \\quad \\text{and} \\quad q + p = 11\n  \\]\n  Solving these equations:\n  \\[\n  2q = 12 \\implies q = 6\n  \\]\n  Hence:\n  \\[\n  p = 5 \\implies s = 5^2 = 25 \\implies n = 7s = 7 \\cdot 25 = 175\n  \\]\n\n#### Case 3: \\(\\gcd(n, 77) = 11\\)\n\n- Let \\( n = 11s \\), where \\( s \\) is not divisible by 7. Then:\n  \\[\n  121s(s + 7) = m^2\n  \\]\n  Since \\( s \\) and \\( s + 7 \\) are coprime, both need to be perfect squares:\n  \\[\n  s = p^2 \\quad \\text{and} \\quad s + 7 = q^2\n  \\]\n  This implies:\n  \\[\n  q^2 - p^2 = 7\n  \\]\n  Using the difference of squares factorization:\n  \\[\n  (q - p)(q + p) = 7\n  \\]\n  The possible pair is \\((1, 7)\\):\n  \\[\n  q - p = 1 \\quad \\text{and} \\quad q + p = 7\n  \\]\n  Solving these equations:\n  \\[\n  2q = 8 \\implies q = 4\n  \\]\n  Hence:\n  \\[\n  p = 3 \\implies s = 3^2 = 9 \\implies n = 11s = 11 \\cdot 9 = 99\n  \\]\n\n#### Case 4: \\(\\gcd(n, 77) = 77\\)\n\n- Let \\( n = 77s \\):\n  \\[\n  77^2 s(s + 1) = m^2\n  \\]\n- Since \\( s \\) and \\( s + 1 \\) are coprime, both need to be perfect squares:\n  \\[\n  s = p^2 \\quad \\text{and} \\quad s + 1 = q^2\n  \\]\n  This implies:\n  \\[\n  q^2 - p^2 = 1\n  \\]\n  Only possibility with natural numbers \\( q > p \\) is:\n  \\[\n  q = p + 1 \\implies n = 77p^2 = 77 \\times 0^2 = 0\n  \\]\n  But \\( n \\) must be a natural number, hence no solution in this case.\n\n### Conclusion:\nThe natural numbers \\( n \\) such that \\( n^2 + 77n \\) is a perfect square are:\n\\(\\boxed{4, 99, 175, 1444}\\)", "domain": "math", "source": "numina_math", "test_cases": null, "starter_code": null}, {"problem": "A board for playing \"Battleship\" has a size of $10 \\times 10$. What is the maximum number of ships with a size of $1 \\times 4$ that can be placed on it?", "deepseek_reasoning": "Okay, let's see. The problem is to find the maximum number of 1x4 ships that can be placed on a 10x10 Battleship board. Hmm. I need to figure out how to arrange as many of these