added to the previous 189 digits would be 189 + 1809 = 1998 digits. Then the next digit, the 1999th, would be the first digit of the 604th three-digit number after 99.\n\nWait, hold on. Let me double-check this.\n\nStarting from 100, the first three-digit number. So:\n\nNumbers 1-9: 9 digits.\n\nNumbers 10-99: 90 numbers *2 digits = 180 digits. Total up to 99: 9+180=189 digits.\n\nNumbers 100 onwards: three digits each.\n\nWe need to find how many three-digit numbers are needed to reach the 1999th digit.\n\nSo from position 190 (start of three-digit numbers) to position 1999: total digits needed here is 1999 - 189 = 1810 digits.\n\nEach three-digit number is 3 digits. So, 1810 divided by 3 is 603 with a remainder of 1 (since 603*3=1809, 1810-1809=1). So, 603 three-digit numbers will take up 1809 digits, bringing us to position 189 + 1809 = 1998. Therefore, the next digit, the 1999th, is the first digit of the next three-digit number, which is the 604th three-digit number after 99.\n\nWait, but what is the 604th three-digit number after 99? The first three-digit number is 100. So, 100 is the first, 101 the second, ..., 100 + 603 = 703. So the 604th three-digit number is 100 + 603 = 703. Therefore, the 1999th digit is the first digit of 703, which is 7.\n\nBut wait, hold on. Let me check again.\n\nWait, starting from 100 as the first three-digit number, then 100 is the 1st, 101 is the 2nd, ..., 100 + n -1 is the nth three-digit number. So, the 604th three-digit number would be 100 + 604 -1 = 703. Therefore, yes, 703 is the number. The digits of 703 are 7, 0, 3. So the first digit is 7, so the 1999th digit is 7.\n\nBut wait, let me confirm step by step:\n\n1. Numbers 1-9: 9 digits. Ends at position 9.\n\n2. Numbers 10-99: 90 numbers, 2 digits each: 180 digits. Ends at position 9 + 180 = 189.\n\n3. Numbers 100 onwards: starting at position 190.\n\nWe need to reach position 1999. How many digits from position 190 to 1999? 1999 - 189 = 1810 digits.\n\nEach three-digit number contributes 3 digits. 1810 \u00f7 3 = 603.333... So, 603 full numbers (603*3=1809 digits) take us to position 189 + 1809 = 1998. The next number is the 604th three-digit number, which is 100 + 603 = 703. The digits of 703 are 7, 0, 3. So the first digit (7) is at position 1999.\n\nTherefore, the answer should be 7.\n\nWait, but let me make sure that we are not making a miscalculation here. Let me verify the counts again.\n\nTotal digits up to 99: 9 + 180 = 189. Correct.\n\nDigits needed after that: 1999 - 189 = 1810. Correct.\n\nNumber of three-digit numbers required: 1810 /3 = 603.333... So, 603 numbers take up 1809 digits, which would end at position 189 + 1809 = 1998. Then, the next digit is the first digit of the next number, which is 703. Hence, 7.\n\nWait, but let me check if 100 + 603 is indeed 703. 100 + 603 = 703. Correct. So 703 is the number. Its digits are 7, 0, 3. Therefore, the first digit is 7, which is at position 1999.\n\nAlternatively, maybe I should list the numbers and count:\n\nAfter 99 comes 100. Let's see:\n\nNumbers from 100 to 702: how many numbers is that?\n\nFrom 100 to 702 inclusive: 702 - 100 +1 = 603 numbers. Each contributes 3 digits, so 603*3=1809 digits. Adding to the previous 189 digits, that's 189 + 1809 = 1998 digits. Then the next number is 703, which starts at position 1999. The first digit of 703 is 7. Therefore, the 1999th digit is 7.\n\nThis seems consistent.\n\nBut wait, let me check another angle. Maybe if I count how many digits each group contributes and see where 1999 falls.\n\nFirst group: 1-9: 9 digits.\n\nSecond group: 10-99: 90 numbers, 2 digits each: 180 digits. Total so far: 189.\n\nThird group: 100-999: 900 numbers, 3 digits each: 2700 digits. Total up to 999: 189 + 2700 = 2889 digits.\n\nFourth group: 1000-1999: 1000 numbers, 4 digits each: 4000 digits. But since we're only going up to 1999, which is 1000 numbers (from 1000 to 1999 inclusive: 1999 - 1000 +1 = 1000 numbers). So 1000*4=4000 digits. Total up to 