composed of five complete quadrilaterals. According to Ptolemy's theorem, each complete quadrilateral has a unique Miquel point.\n\n3. **Establishing the Cyclic Natures of Points**:\n   - Set \\(B_{2} A_{2}\\) extended to meet \\(C_{1} B_{4}\\) extended at point \\(D\\).\n   - From the properties of cyclic quadrilaterals, observe that triangles \\(A_{5} C_{1} C_{4}\\) and \\(C_{4} A_{5} C_{2}\\) both have circumcircles passing through \\(B_{1}\\) and \\(B_{4}\\). Thus \\(B_{1}, B_{4}, C_{1}, C_{4}\\) are concyclic.\n   - Similarly, quadrilateral \\(A_{2}B_{2}C_{4}B_{1}\\) is cyclic, implying \\(\\angle B_{1}B_{4}D = \\angle 1 = \\angle 2 = \\angle 3\\).\n\n   This shows \\( \\angle \\text{at } D \\) and points \\(B_{1}, B_{2}, B_{4}, D\\) are concyclic.\n\n4. **Interconnecting Points**:\n   - Determine that quadrilateral \\(A_{2} B_{2} A_{3} B_{3}\\) also implies \\(\\angle A_{2}B_{2}A_{3} = \\angle 5 = \\angle 7\\).\n   - From quadrilateral \\(A_{3} B_{3} C_{1} B_{4}\\) being cyclic, we get \\(\\angle C_1 A_3 B_3 = \\angle 6 = \\angle 8\\).\n\n5. **Summing the Angles**:\n   - Sum of internal angles should equal \\(180^{\\circ}\\):\n     \\[\n     \\angle D + \\angle B_{3} = \\angle 4 + \\angle 5 + \\angle 6 = \\angle 4 + \\angle 7 + \\angle 8 = 180^{\\circ}\n     \\]\n\n   Hence, \\(B_{2}, B_{3}, B_{4}, D\\) are concyclic, indicating \\(B_{1}, B_{2}, B_{3}, B_{4}, D\\) are on the same circle.\n\n6. **Extending to Remaining Points**:\n   - Similarly, we can extend this argument to show that \\(B_{2}, B_{3}, B_{4}, B_{5}\\) are cyclic.\n   - This concludes \\(B_{1}, B_{2}, B_{3}, B_{4}, B_{5}\\) are all cyclic.\n\n### Conclusion: \n\\[\n\\boxed{B_{1}, B_{2}, B_{3}, B_{4}, B_{5} \\text{ are concyclic.}}\n\\]", "domain": "math", "source": "numina_math", "test_cases": null, "starter_code": null}, {"problem": "The sea includes a bay with saltier water. The salinity of the water in the sea is 120 per mille, in the bay it is 240 per mille, and the salinity of the part of the sea excluding the bay is 110 per mille. By how many times is the volume of water in the sea greater than the volume of water in the bay? The volume of water is considered including the volume of salt. Per mille is one thousandth of a number; salinity is defined as the ratio of the volume of salt to the total volume of the mixture.", "deepseek_reasoning": "Okay, let's see. So the problem is about comparing the volume of the sea and the bay based on their salinity. Hmm. The sea has a bay with saltier water. The salinity of the sea is 120 per mille, the bay is 240 per mille, and the rest of the sea (excluding the bay) is 110 per mille. We need to find how many times the volume of the sea is greater than the volume of the bay. \n\nFirst, let me make sure I understand the terms. Salinity here is defined as the ratio of the volume of salt to the total volume of the mixture, measured in per mille (which is parts per thousand). So, for example, a salinity of 120 per mille means that 120 parts out of 1000 are salt, and the rest is water. Wait, but actually, the problem says \"the volume of water is considered including the volume of salt.\" Wait, that might be a translation issue. Let me read that again. \n\n\"The volume of water is considered including the volume of salt. Per mille is one thousandth of a number; salinity is defined as the ratio of the volume of salt to the total volume of the mixture.\"\n\nOkay, so salinity is (volume of salt)/(total volume of mixture), and the total volume includes both water and salt. So when they say \"volume of water,\" they actually mean the total volume of the mixture (water + salt). That's a bit confusing because in common terms, water volume might exclude salt, but here it's specified to include salt. So when they mention the volume of water, it's the total volume. Got it.\n\nSo the sea as a whole has a salinity of 120 per mille. The bay, which is part of the sea, has a higher salinity of 240 per mille. The rest of the sea (excluding the bay) has a lower salinity of 110 per mille. We need to find the ratio of the total volume of the sea (including the bay) to the volume of the bay.\n\nLet me denote some variables to make this clearer.\n\nLet\u2019s let:\n- V_sea = total volume of the sea (including the bay)\n- V_bay = volume of the bay\n- V_rest = volume of the sea excluding the bay = V_sea - V_bay\n\nGiven:\n- Salinity of the entire sea (including bay): 120 per mille = 0.120\n- Salinity of the bay: 240 per mille = 0.240\n- Salinity of the rest of the sea: 110 per mille = 0.110\n\nSince salinity is the ratio of salt volume to total volume, the total amount of salt in each part can be calculated as salinity multiplied by the volume of that part.\n\nSo, total salt in the sea = total salt in the bay + total salt in the rest of the sea.\n\nMathematically:\n0.120 * V_sea = 0.240 * V_bay + 0.110 * (V_sea - V_bay)\n\nLet me write that equation again:\n\n0.120 V_sea = 0.240 V_bay + 0.110 (V_sea - V_bay)\n\nOur goal is to find V_sea / V_bay.\n\nLet\u2019s solve for V_sea in terms of V_bay.\n\nFirst, expand the right-hand side:\n\n0.240 V_bay + 0.110 V_sea - 0.110 V_bay\n\nCombine like terms:\n\n(0.240 - 0.110) V_bay + 0.110 V_sea\n\nWhich is:\n\n0.130 V_bay + 0.110 V_sea\n\nSo now the equation is:\n\n0.120 V_sea = 0.130 V_bay + 0.110 V_sea\n\nSubtract 0.110 V_sea from both sides:\n\n0.120 V_sea - 0.110 V_sea = 0.130 V_bay\n\nWhich simplifies to:\n\n0.010 V_sea = 0.130 V_bay\n\nDivide both sides by 0.010:\n\nV_sea = (0.130 / 0.010) V_bay\n\nCalculate 0.130 divided by 0.010:\n\n0.130 / 0.010 = 13\n\nTherefore:\n\nV_sea = 13 V_bay\n\nSo the volume of the sea is 13 times the volume of the bay. Therefore, the answer is 13.\n\nWait, let me check my steps again to make sure I didn't make a mistake.\n\nStart with the salt balance equation:\n\nTotal salt in sea = total salt in bay + total salt in rest of sea.\n\n0.120 V_sea = 0.240 V_bay + 0.110 (V_sea - V_bay)\n\nExpand the right side:\n\n0.240 V_bay + 0.110 V_sea - 0.110 V_bay = (0.240 - 0.110) V_bay + 0.110 V_sea = 0.130 V_bay + 0.110 V_sea\n\nLeft side is 0.120 V_sea.\n\nSo:\n\n0.120 V_sea = 0.130 V_bay + 0.110 V_sea\n\nSubtract 0.110 V_sea from both sides:\n\n0.010 V_sea = 0.130 V_bay\n\nThen:\n\nV_sea / V_bay = 0.130 / 0.010 = 13\n\nYes, that looks correct. So the total volume of the sea is 13 times that of the bay. Therefore, the answer is 13 times.\n\nBut let me think again to ensure there are no errors. Sometimes when dealing with per mille, it's easy to miscalculate decimal places. Let me verify the division:\n\n0.130 divided by 0.010. Moving the decimal three places: 0.130 / 0.010 is equivalent to 130 / 10 = 13. Yep, correct.\n\nAlternatively, 0.130 is 13% and 0.010 is 1%, so 13% / 1% = 13. Correct.\n\nAnother way: Think in per mille terms. 0.130 is 130 per mille, 0.010 is 10 per mille. 130 / 10 = 13. Same result.\n\nTherefore, the calculation is correct. So the volume of the sea is 13 times the volume of the bay.", "deepseek_solution": "The problem requires determining how many times the volume of the sea is greater than the volume of the bay, given their salinities. Here's the systematic solution:\n\n1. **Define Variables:**\n   - Let \\( V_{\\text{sea}} \\) = Total volume of the sea (including the bay).\n   - Let \\( V_{\\text{bay}} \\) = Volume of the bay.\n   - The remaining sea volume is \\( V_{\\text{rest}} = V_{\\text{sea}} - V_{\\text{bay}} \\).\n\n2. **Given Salinities:**\n   - Entire sea: \\( 120 \\, \\text{per mille} = 0.120 \\).\n   - Bay: \\( 240 \\, \\text{per mille} = 0.240 \\).\n   - Remaining sea: \\( 110 \\, \\text{per mille} = 0.110 \\).\n\n3. **Salt Balance Equation:**\n   Total salt in the sea equals the sum of salt in the bay and the remaining sea:\n   \\[\n   0.120 V_{\\text{sea}} = 0.240 V_{\\text{bay}} + 0.110 (V_{\\text{sea}} - V_{\\text{bay}}).\n   \\]\n\n4. **Expand and Simplify:**\n   \\[\n   0.120 V_{\\text{sea}} = 0.240 V_{\\text{bay}} + 0.110 V_{\\text{sea}} - 0.110 V_{\\text{bay}}.\n   \\]\n   Combine like terms:\n   \\[\n   0.120 V_{\\text{sea}} = 0.130 V_{\\text{bay}} + 0.110 V_{\\text{sea}}.\n   \\]\n   Subtract \\( 0.110 V_{\\text{sea}} \\) from both sides:\n   \\[\n   0.010 V_{\\text{sea}} = 0.130 V_{\\text{bay}}.\n   \\]\n   Solve for \\( V_{\\text{sea}} / V_{\\text{bay}} \\):\n   \\[\n   \\frac{V_{\\text{sea}}}{V_{\\text{bay}}} = \\frac{0.130}{0.010} = 13.\n   \\]\n\n**Final Answer:** The volume of the sea is **13 times** greater than the volume of the bay.", "ground_truth_solution": "\nTo solve this problem, we need to determine the ratio of the volume of the sea to the volume of the bay, given the salinity levels and the definition of salinity. \n\n1. Let the volume of salt in the bay be \\(s_{1}\\) and the volume of water in the bay be \\(v_{1}\\). Similarly, let the volume of salt in the sea (excluding the bay) be \\(s_{2}\\) and the volume of water in that part be \\(v_{2}\\).\n\n2. According to the given information, we have the following equations for salinity:\n   \\[\n   \\frac{s_{1}}{v_{1}} = \\frac{240}{1000} \\quad \\text{(salinity of the bay)}\n   \\]\n   \\[\n   \\frac{s_{2}}{v_{2}} = \\frac{110}{1000} \\quad \\text{(salinity of the sea excluding the bay)}\n   \\]\n   \\[\n   \\frac{s_{1} + s_{2}}{v_{1} + v_{2}} = \\frac{120}{1000} \\quad \\text{(salinity of the entire sea)}\n   \\]\n\n3. We first derive the expressions for \\(s_{1}\\) and \\(s_{2}\\) from the salinity formulas:\n   \\[\n   s_{1} = \\frac{240}{1000} v_{1} = 0.24 v_{1}\n   \\]\n   \\[\n   s_{2} = \\frac{110}{1000} v_{2} = 0.11 v_{2}\n   \\]\n\n4. Substitute \\(s_{1}\\) and \\(s_{2}\\) into the equation for the entire sea salinity:\n   \\[\n   \\frac{0.24 v_{1} + 0.11 v_{2}}{v_{1} + v_{2}} = \\frac{120}{1000}\n   \\]\n   Simplifying the right side, we get:\n   \\[\n   \\frac{0.24 v_{1} + 0.11 v_{2}}{v_{1} + v_{2}} = 0.12\n   \\]\n\n5. Clear the fraction by multiplying both sides by \\(v_{1} + v_{2}\\):\n   \\[\n   0.24 v_{1} + 0.11 v_{2} = 0.12 (v_{1} + v_{2})\n   \\]\n\n6. Distribute the \\(0.12\\) on the right-hand side:\n   \\[\n   0.24 v_{1} + 0.11 v_{2} = 0.12 v_{1} + 0.12 v_{2}\n   \\]\n\n7. Rearrange to group terms involving \\(v_{1}\\) and \\(v_{2}\\) on opposite sides:\n   \\[\n   0.24 v_{1} - 0.12 v_{1} = 0.12 v_{2} - 0.11 v_{2}\n   \\]\n\n8. Simplify the coefficients:\n   \\[\n   0.12 v_{1} = 0.01 v_{2}\n   \\]\n\n9. Solve for \\(v_{2}\\) in terms of \\(v_{1}\\):\n   \\[\n   v_{2} = \\frac{0.12}{0.01} v_{1} = 12 v_{1}\n   \\]\n\n10. The volume of the entire sea \\(V\\) including the bay is \\(v_{1} + v_{2}\\):\n    \\[\n    V = v_{1} + v_{2} = v_{1} + 12 v_{1} = 13 v_{1}\n    \\]\n\n11. Finally, the ratio of the volume of the entire sea (including the bay) to the volume of the bay is:\n    \\[\n    \\frac{V}{v_{1}} = \\frac{13 v_{1}}{v_{1}} = 13\n    \\]\n\n### Conclusion:\n\\[ \\boxed{13} \\]", "domain": "math", "source": "numina_math", "test_cases": null, "starter_code": null}, {"problem": "Given the following sign table:\n\n$$\n\\begin{array}{llll}\n+ & + & + & - \\\\\n- & + & + & - \\\\\n+ & + & + & - \\\\\n+ & - & - & -\n\\end{array}\n$$\n\nAn operation consists of inverting all the signs in a row or a column. Can we achieve a table with only + signs after a certain sequence of operations?", "deepseek_reasoning": "Okay, let's try to figure out if we can turn this table into all plus signs by flipping rows or columns. So the original table is:\n\nRow 1: + + + -\nRow 2: - + + -\nRow 3: + + + -\nRow 4: + - - -\n\nEach operation is flipping all signs in a row or a column. So flipping a row would change all its + to - and vice versa. Same for a column. The goal is to see if after some number of these operations, we can make every cell in the table a +.\n\nFirst, maybe I should visualize the table. Let me write it out again:\n\n1: + + + -\n2: - + + -\n3: + + + -\n4: + - - -\n\nLooking at this, the first thing I notice is that rows 1, 3, and 4 have three + and one -, except row 2 starts with a -. Columns: Let's check the columns.\n\nColumn 1: +, -, +, + \u2192 three + and one -.\nColumn 2: +, +, +, - \u2192 three + and one -.\nColumn 3: +, +, +, - \u2192 same as column 2.\nColumn 4: -, -, -, - \u2192 all four are -.\n\nSo column 4 is all minuses. That's probably a key point. If we flip column 4, that would turn all those minuses into pluses. Let's try that first.\n\nAfter flipping column 4:\n\nRow 1: + + + +\nRow 2: - + + +\nRow 3: + + + +\nRow 4: + - - +\n\nNow column 4 is all +. But now rows 2 and 4 have some minuses. Let's check the new table:\n\n1: + + + +\n2: - + + +\n3: + + + +\n4: + - - +\n\nSo the problem now is row 2 has a - in column 1, and row 4 has minuses in columns 2 and 3.\n\nMaybe flip row 2? If we flip row 2, the first element becomes +, and the rest were already + except the first. Wait, flipping row 2 would change all its signs. Original row 2 after flipping column 4 was -, +, +, +. Flipping row 2 would make it +, -, -, -. Wait, but that's bad because it introduces more minuses. Hmm, maybe not.\n\nAlternatively, maybe flip column 1? Column 1 has entries: +, -, +, +. If we flip column 1, it becomes -, +, -, -. Then the table would be:\n\nRow 1: - + + +\nRow 2: + + + +\nRow 3: - + + +\nRow 4: - - - +\n\nBut now column 1 has three minuses and one +. That seems worse. Maybe that's not the way.\n\nWait, maybe instead of flipping column 4 first, let's see another approach. Since column 4 is all -, flipping it is necessary. Because otherwise, we can't get rid of those minuses. Because flipping rows would only affect their own column 4 entries. Let's see: if we don't flip column 4, then each of the original column 4 entries are -, so to turn them into +, we have to flip each row that has a - in column 4. But rows 1, 2, 3 all have - in column 4. So flipping rows 1, 2, 3 would turn their column 4 entries to +. But flipping those rows would affect other columns as well.\n\nOriginal rows:\n\nRow 1: + + + -\nRow 2: - + + -\nRow 3: + + + -\nRow 4: + - - -\n\nIf we flip rows 1, 2, 3:\n\nFlipped row 1: - - - +\nFlipped row 2: + - - +\nFlipped row 3: - - - +\nRow 4 remains: + - - -\n\nNow column 4 entries would be +, +, +, -, which is better but not all +. So we still need to flip row 4 to make its column 4 entry +. But flipping row 4 would turn its entries to - + + +. Let's check:\n\nAfter flipping rows 1, 2, 3, and 4:\n\nRow 1: - - - +\nRow 2: + - - +\nRow 3: - - - +\nRow 4: - + + +\n\nNow column 4 is all + (since row 4's column 4 is now +). But the rest of the columns might have issues. Let's check all columns:\n\nColumn 1: -, +, -, - \u2192 four entries: three -, one +.\nColumn 2: -, -, -, + \u2192 three -, one +.\nColumn 3: -, -, -, + \u2192 same as column 2.\nColumn 4: all +.\n\nSo this approach leads to columns 1-3 having mostly minuses. Not good. So flipping those rows causes other columns to have minuses. Maybe flipping columns is better.\n\nAlternatively, going back to the initial idea: flip column 4 first. Then column 4 is all +. Then we have the table as:\n\nRow 1: + + + +\nRow 2: - + + +\nRow 3: + + + +\nRow 4: + - - +\n\nNow, the remaining minuses are in row 2 (column 1) and row 4 (columns 2 and 3). Let's see. If we can flip those individually, but we can only flip entire rows or columns.\n\nTo fix row 2's column 1, flipping column 1 would affect all rows. Column 1 currently has entries: +, -, +, +. Flipping column 1 would make it -, +, -, -. Then:\n\nRow 1: - + + +\nRow 2: + + + +\nRow 3: - + + +\nRow 4: - - - +\n\nNow, column 1 has -, +, -, - (three -, one +). Row 4 now has - in column 1, and columns 2-4: -, -, +. So row 4 becomes: -, -, -, +. Wait, no. Let's correct:\n\nOriginal after flipping column 4 and then flipping column 1:\n\nRow 1: - (flipped from +) + + + \u2192 -, +, +, +\nRow 2: + (flipped from -) + + + \u2192 +, +, +, +\nRow 3: - (flipped from +) + + + \u2192 -, +, +, +\nRow 4: - (flipped from +) - - + \u2192 -, -, -, +\n\nSo now, rows 1 and 3 have a - in column 1, and row 4 has minuses in columns 1, 2, 3. Column 1: -, +, -, - (three -). Columns 2-4: mostly + except row 4. This seems worse. So flipping column 1 introduced more minuses.\n\nAlternatively, instead of flipping column 1, flip row 2. Original after flipping column 4:\n\nRow 1: + + + +\nRow 2: - + + +\nRow 3: + + + +\nRow 4: + - - +\n\nIf we flip row 2, which is -, +, +, +, it becomes +, -, -, -.\n\nNow the table becomes:\n\nRow 1: + + + +\nRow 2: + - - -\nRow 3: + + + +\nRow 4: + - - +\n\nNow, looking at columns:\n\nColumn 1: all + (rows 1-4: +, +, +, +) \u2192 good.\nColumn 2: +, -, +, - \u2192 two + and two -.\nColumn 3: +, -, +, - \u2192 same as column 2.\nColumn 4: +, -, +, + \u2192 three + and one -.\n\nSo column 4 now has a - in row 2. So we need to fix columns 2, 3, and 4.\n\nHmm. Let's see. If we flip column 2, which is +, -, +, -, flipping gives -, +, -, +. Then column 2 becomes:\n\nRow 1: -, Row 2: +, Row 3: -, Row 4: +.\n\nBut then column 2 would be alternating. Not sure if helpful.\n\nAlternatively, flip column 4 again. But column 4 was flipped once already. Flipping it again would bring back the original minuses. Not helpful.\n\nAlternatively, flip row 4. Current row 4 is +, -, -, +. Flipping it gives -, +, +, -. Then:\n\nRow 4 becomes -, +, +, -. But this introduces a - in column 1 and 4. Column 1 was all +, now row 4 has -, which is bad. Column 4 now has -, which was previously +. So that's worse.\n\nAlternatively, flip columns 2 and 3. Let's see. Flipping column 2:\n\nOriginal column 2 entries: +, -, +, -. Flipping gives -, +, -, +.\n\nResulting table:\n\nRow 1: +, -, +, +\nRow 2: +, +, +, -\nRow 3: +, -, +, +\nRow 4: +, +, -, +\n\nNow columns:\n\nColumn 1: all +.\nColumn 2: -, +, -, +.\nColumn 3: +, +, +, -.\nColumn 4: +, -, +, +.\n\nNow column 3 has a - in row 4. Not better. Similarly for column 4.\n\nThis seems messy. Maybe there's a different approach.\n\nWait, maybe the key is to realize that flipping a row or column twice is equivalent to doing nothing. So each row and column can be flipped 0 or 1 times. So the problem reduces to finding a set of rows and columns to flip such that all entries become +.\n\nThis is similar to solving a system of equations where each flip toggles the sign. Since each flip is its own inverse (doing it twice cancels out), it's a linear algebra problem over GF(2), but with signs instead of bits. But maybe there's a parity consideration.\n\nAlternatively, think of each cell as needing an even or odd number of flips to become +. Since starting from some state, each flip (row or column) affects the cell. So for each cell, the total number of flips affecting it must be even if it's already +, or odd if it's -.\n\nWait, but this might not be the case. Let's think: each cell starts as either + or -. To reach +, the number of flips affecting it must be even if it's + (so that it remains +), or odd if it's - (to flip it to +). Each row flip and column flip affects all cells in that row or column. So the problem is equivalent to solving a system where for each cell (i,j), the sum of row flips for row i and column flips for column j must be congruent to 1 (mod 2) if the cell is -, and 0 (mod 2) if the cell is +.\n\nBut wait, actually, when you flip a row or column, you invert the sign. So starting from the initial state, each flip toggles the sign. Therefore, for each cell, the total number of times it's flipped (row flips + column flips) must be odd if it was originally -, and even if it was originally +, to end up as +.\n\nTherefore, we can model this as a system of linear equations over GF(2). Let me define variables:\n\nLet r1, r2, r3, r4 be 1 if we flip row 1, 2, 3, 4 respectively, 0 otherwise.\n\nSimilarly, c1, c2, c3, c4 be 1 if we flip column 1, 2, 3, 4 respectively, 0 otherwise.\n\nFor each cell (i,j), the equation is:\n\nr_i + c_j \u2261 t_{i,j} (mod 2)\n\nwhere t_{i,j} is 1 if the original cell (i,j) is -, 0 if it's +.\n\nBecause to turn the original cell into +, if it was -, we need an odd number of flips (r_i + c_j \u2261 1), and if it was +, even number of flips (r_i + c_j \u2261 0).\n\nSo we need to solve this system for r1, r2, r3, r4, c1, c2, c3, c4.\n\nLet me write down the equations based on the original table:\n\nOriginal table:\n\nRow 1: + + + - \u2192 cells (1,1)=+, (1,2)=+, (1,3)=+, (1,4)=-\nRow 2: - + + - \u2192 (2,1)=-, (2,2)=+, (2,3)=+, (2,4)=-\nRow 3: + + + - \u2192 (3,1)=+, (3,2)=+, (3,3)=+, (3,4)=-\nRow 4: + - - - \u2192 (4,1)=+, (4,2)=-, (4,3)=-, (4,4)=-\n\nTherefore, t_{i,j} = 1 where cell (i,j) is -, else 0.\n\nSo equations:\n\nFor each cell (i,j):\n\nr_i + c_j = t_{i,j} mod 2\n\nSo let's list all 16 equations (4 rows x 4 columns):\n\nFor row 1:\n\n1. r1 + c1 = 0 (since (1,1) is +)\n2. r1 + c2 = 0\n3. r1 + c3 = 0\n4. r1 + c4 = 1 (since (1,4) is -)\n\nRow 2:\n\n5. r2 + c1 = 1 (since (2,1) is -)\n6. r2 + c2 = 0\n7. r2 + c3 = 0\n8. r2 + c4 = 1\n\nRow 3:\n\n9. r3 + c1 = 0\n10. r3 + c2 = 0\n11. r3 + c3 = 0\n12. r3 + c4 = 1\n\nRow 4:\n\n13. r4 + c1 = 0\n14. r4 + c2 = 1 (since (4,2) is -)\n15. r4 + c3 = 1\n16. r4 + c4 = 1\n\nNow, we have 16 equations with 8 variables (r1-r4, c1-c4). However, these equations are not all independent. Let's try to solve the system.\n\nFirst, notice that for each row, the equations for columns 1-4 can be used to express r_i in terms of c_j. For example, from row 1:\n\nEquations 1-4:\n\nr1 + c1 = 0 \u21d2 r1 = c1\nr1 + c2 = 0 \u21d2 c1 + c2 = 0 \u21d2 c2 = c1\nr1 + c3 = 0 \u21d2 c1 + c3 = 0 \u21d2 c3 = c1\nr1 + c4 = 1 \u21d2 c1 + c4 = 1 \u21d2 c4 = 1 + c1\n\nSo from row 1, we can express c2, c3, c4 in terms of c1:\n\nc2 = c1\n\nc3 = c1\n\nc4 = 1 + c1\n\nSimilarly, for row 2:\n\nEquations 5-8:\n\nr2 + c1 = 1 \u21d2 r2 = 1 + c1\n\nr2 + c2 = 0 \u21d2 (1 + c1) + c2 = 0 \u21d2 c2 = 1 + c1\n\nBut from row 1, c2 = c1. Therefore:\n\nc1 = 1 + c1 \u21d2 0 = 1. Contradiction. Hmm, that's a problem.\n\nWait, this suggests that the equations may be inconsistent. Let's check step by step.\n\nFrom row 1:\n\nc2 = c1\n\nc3 = c1\n\nc4 = 1 + c1\n\nFrom row 2:\n\nr2 = 1 + c1 (from equation 5)\n\nFrom equation 6: r2 + c2 = 0 \u21d2 (1 + c1) + c2 = 0. But c2 = c1, so:\n\n1 + c1 + c1 = 1 + 0 = 1 = 0 mod 2 \u21d2 1 \u2261 0 mod 2. Which is impossible.\n\nThis contradiction means that there's no solution. Therefore, it's impossible to flip rows and columns to make all + signs.\n\nBut wait, maybe I made a mistake in the equations. Let's double-check.\n\nOriginal cell (2,1) is -, so t_{2,1}=1. So equation 5: r2 + c1 =1.\n\nFrom row 1, we have c2 = c1, c3 = c1, c4=1 + c1.\n\nFrom row 2, equation 5 gives r2=1 + c1.\n\nEquation 6: r2 + c2 =0 \u21d2 (1 + c1) + c2 =0. But c2 = c1, so:\n\n1 + c1 + c1 =1 +0=1=0 mod2 \u21d2 1=0. Contradiction.\n\nTherefore, the system is inconsistent. Hence, no solution exists. Therefore, it's impossible to achieve all + signs.\n\nBut wait, let's try another approach. Maybe the initial assumption is wrong. Let's try to see if there's a different way.\n\nAlternatively, notice that flipping a row and then a column is the same as flipping the column and then the row. The order doesn't matter because flipping is commutative. So the total effect is the same.\n\nThe key is that each cell is flipped once for each row and column flip it's in. So the parity (even or odd) of flips is what matters.\n\nAnother way to think about it: the problem is equivalent to solving the system of equations modulo 2, as above. Since we arrived at a contradiction, there's no solution.\n\nAlternatively, notice that the number of - signs in the table is important. Each flip changes the number of - signs by an even or odd number.\n\nOriginal table:\n\nRow 1: 1 -\nRow 2: 2 -'s (columns 1 and 4)\nRow 3: 1 -\nRow 4: 3 -'s (columns 2,3,4)\nTotal -'s: 1 +2 +1 +3=7.\n\nEach row flip changes the number of -'s by (number of +'s - number of -'s) in that row. Similarly for column flip.\n\nBut since we want to reach 0 -'s, which is even, and we start with 7 (odd), but each operation changes the number of -'s by an even number (flipping a row with k -'s turns them into k +'s, so the change is 4 - 2k, which is even. Similarly for columns). But 7 is odd, and each operation changes the total number of -'s by an even number. So the parity of the total number of -'s is invariant modulo 2. Since we start with 7 (odd), and each operation changes it by even, we can never reach 0 (even). Therefore, it's impossible.\n\nAh, this is another way to see it. The total number of - signs is initially 7, which is odd. Each operation flips an even number of signs (since flipping a row flips 4 signs, even number; flipping a column flips 4 signs, also even). The total number of - signs must remain odd because flipping an even number of signs changes the total by an even number. But 0 is even, so we can't reach it from an odd number. Hence, impossible.\n\nTherefore, the answer is no, we cannot achieve a table with only + signs.", "deepseek_solution": "To determine if we can transform the given table into one with only '+' signs through row or column inversions, we analyze the problem using two key approaches: linear algebra over GF(2) and parity considerations.\n\n### Key Steps:\n\n1. **Linear System Approach**:\n   - Model each cell's requirement as equations where flipping a row \\( r_i \\) or column \\( c_j \\) toggles the sign. For each cell \\((i,j)\\), the equation is \\( r_i + c_j \\equiv t_{i,j} \\mod 2 \\), where \\( t_{i,j} = 1 \\) if the original cell is '-', else 0.\n   - From the original table, derive 16 equations. Solving these leads to contradictions (e.g., \\( 1 \\equiv 0 \\mod 2 \\)), indicating the system is inconsistent and no solution exists.\n\n2. **Parity Argument**:\n   - The initial table has 7 '-' signs (odd count). Each row or column flip changes an even number of signs, preserving the parity of total '-' signs. Since transitioning from an odd to even count is impossible via even changes, achieving all '+' (0 '-'s) is unattainable.\n\n### Conclusion:\nBoth methods confirm that transforming the table into all '+' signs is impossible due to inherent inconsistencies and parity constraints.\n\n**Answer:** No, it is not possible to achieve a table with only '+' signs through any sequence of row or column inversions.", "ground_truth_solution": "\n1. **Definition of \\( m \\)**:\n   Let \\( m \\) be the number of negative signs \\( (-) \\) in the initial table:\n   \\[\n   \\begin{array}{llll}\n   + & + & + & - \\\\\n   - & + & + & - \\\\\n   + & + & + & - \\\\\n   + & - & - & -\n   \\end{array}\n   \\]\n\n2. **Initial Count of Negative Signs**:\n   Counting the negative signs, we have:\n   \\[\n   \\begin{aligned}\n   &1 \\text{ (first row)} \\\\\n   &2 \\text{ (second row)} \\\\\n   &1 \\text{ (third row)} \\\\\n   &3 \\text{ (fourth row)} \\\\\n   \\end{aligned}\n   \\]\n   Thus, the total number of negative signs, \\( m \\), is:\n   \\[\n   m = 1 + 2 + 1 + 3 = 7\n   \\]\n\n3. **Parity of \\( m \\)**:\n   Notice that \\( m = 7 \\), which is odd.\n\n4. **Effect of an Operation**:\n   An operation involves inverting all the signs in a row or a column. Let's examine the effect of an operation on the parity of \\( m \\):\n   - If a row or column contains \\( n \\) negative signs, then it contains \\( 4 - n \\) positive signs (since there are 4 signs per row/column).\n   - Upon inverting the signs in a row or column, the number of negative signs \\( n \\) is replaced by \\( 4 - n \\).\n\n5. **Change in \\( m \\)**:\n   Let the initial number of negative signs be \\( m \\) and suppose we invert a row (or column) with \\( n \\) negative signs:\n   \\[\n   m_{\\text{new}} = m - n + (4 - n) = m - 2n + 4\n   \\]\n   Simplifying modulo 2:\n   \\[\n   m_{\\text{new}} \\equiv (m - 2n + 4) \\pmod{2}\n   \\]\n   \\[\n   m_{\\text{new}} \\equiv m + 0 \\pmod{2}\n   \\]\n   Hence, the parity of \\( m \\) remains unchanged:\n   \\[\n   m_{\\text{new}} \\equiv m \\pmod{2}\n   \\]\n\n6. **Conclusion**:\n   Given \\( m \\) is initially odd and the parity of \\( m \\) cannot be changed by the operations defined, \\( m \\) will remain odd. Since \\( 0 \\) is even, \\( m \\) can never be reduced to zero by any sequence of operations.\n\nTherefore, it is impossible to transform the table to contain only positive signs \\( (+) \\).\n\n\\[\n\\boxed{No}\n\\]", "domain": "math", "source": "numina_math", "test_cases": null, "starter_code": null}, {"problem": "Consider the sequence\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\nx_{1} & =19, \\\\\nx_{2} & =95, \\\\\nx_{n+2} & =\\operatorname{lcm}\\left(x_{n+1}, x_{n}\\right)+x_{n},\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nfor \\(n > 1\\), where \\(\\operatorname{lcm}(a, b)\\) means the least common multiple of \\(a\\) and \\(b\\). Find the greatest common divisor of \\(x_{1995}\\) and \\(x_{1996}\\).", "deepseek_reasoning": "Okay, let's try to figure out this problem. We have a sequence defined by x\u2081 = 19, x\u2082 = 95, and then for n > 1, x_{n+2} is the least common multiple of x_{n+1} and x_n plus x_n. We need to find the greatest common divisor (GCD) of x_{1995} and x_{1996}. \n\nFirst, let me make sure I understand the sequence. The first two terms are 19 and 95. Then each subsequent term is the LCM of the two previous terms plus the immediately preceding term. So, for example, x\u2083 would be LCM(x\u2082, x\u2081) + x\u2081. Let me compute the first few terms to see if I can spot a pattern or some recurring relationship.\n\nStarting with x\u2081 = 19 and x\u2082 = 95. Let's compute x\u2083:\n\nx\u2083 = LCM(95, 19) + 19. \n\nSince 95 is a multiple of 19 (19*5=95), the LCM of 95 and 19 is 95. So x\u2083 = 95 + 19 = 114.\n\nNow x\u2083 is 114. Next, x\u2084 = LCM(114, 95) + 95. Let's compute LCM(114, 95). \n\nFirst, factorize both numbers:\n\n95 = 5 * 19\n\n114 = 2 * 3 * 19\n\nSo LCM is the product of the highest powers of all primes present: 2 * 3 * 5 * 19 = 570. Then x\u2084 = 570 + 95 = 665.\n\nNext, x\u2085 = LCM(665, 114) + 114. Let's factorize 665 and 114:\n\n665 = 5 * 7 * 19\n\n114 = 2 * 3 * 19\n\nSo LCM is 2 * 3 