ce, even a small \u03b8_i can create a large cap. For instance, if the apex is at distance d = R - \u03b5 from the center, very close to the sphere. Then, the cone with half-angle \u03b8_i will intersect the sphere in a cap whose area depends on both \u03b8_i and d. For small \u03b5, even a small \u03b8_i can lead to a large cap. Hence, the area A_i could be much larger than 2\u03c0R\u00b2(1 - cos\u03b8_i), violating the earlier assumption.\n\nTherefore, the previous proof is incorrect because it assumed A_i \u2264 2\u03c0R\u00b2(1 - cos\u03b8_i), which isn't true when the apex is close to the sphere. So we need a different approach.\n\nLet\u2019s think differently. Maybe use a different method inspired by the mean squared angle. Let's consider a unit sphere and integrate some function over all directions, relating to the cones covering the sphere.\n\nSuppose for each cone C_i with apex at some point and half-angle \u03b8_i, we can associate a function f_i(u) which is 1 if direction u is covered by C_i and 0 otherwise. Since the cones cover the sphere, sum_i f_i(u) \u2265 1 for all u.\n\nIntegrate both sides over the unit sphere:\n\n\u222b_{S\u00b2} sum_i f_i(u) d\u03a9 \u2265 \u222b_{S\u00b2} 1 d\u03a9 = 4\u03c0.\n\nExchange sum and integral:\n\nsum_i \u222b_{S\u00b2} f_i(u) d\u03a9 \u2265 4\u03c0.\n\nEach integral \u222b_{S\u00b2} f_i(u) d\u03a9 is the solid angle of the cone C_i as viewed from its apex. For a cone with half-angle \u03b8_i, the solid angle is \u03a9_i = 2\u03c0(1 - cos\u03b8_i). However, the solid angle depends on the position of the apex relative to the sphere. If the apex is at the center, then yes, it's 2\u03c0(1 - cos\u03b8_i). But if the apex is elsewhere, the solid angle as viewed from the apex is still 2\u03c0(1 - cos\u03b8_i), but the mapping to the unit sphere around the origin is different. Wait, no. The solid angle is a property of the cone from its apex, but when we integrate over the unit sphere at the origin, the coverage f_i(u) depe