a measure proportional to \u03b8_i\u00b2, leading to the sum over \u03b8_i\u00b2 being bounded below.\n\nAlternatively, consider that the set of directions around each cone's axis must cover the sphere of directions. For each cone, the set of directions it covers is a spherical cap of angular radius \u03b8_i. To cover the sphere with these caps, the sum of their solid angles must be at least the solid angle of the sphere. However, this is similar to the earlier approach but requires the caps to be on the same sphere.\n\nBut the cones have different apexes, so their directional coverages are from different points. This complicates things because the same direction in space might be covered by different cones from different apexes.\n\nWait, but if we fix a direction vector v, then there must be some cone such that when you move along v from its apex, you stay within the cone. But since the cone is infinite, once you are in the cone, you are covered in that direction from the apex. So for direction v, there must be an apex point P such that the line starting at P in direction v is contained in the cone. This implies that the direction v is within the cone's angular radius \u03b8_i from the cone's axis.\n\nTherefore, for each direction v, there exists a cone C_i with axis direction u_i (unit vector) such that the angle between u_i and v is at most \u03b8_i. Therefore, the cones' directional coverage must cover the entire sphere of directions. Thus, the problem reduces to covering the sphere with angular caps of radii \u03b8_i, and we need to show that sum \u03b8_i\u00b2 \u2265 4 (then sum \u03c6_i\u00b2 = sum (2\u03b8_i)\u00b2 = 4 sum \u03b8_i\u00b2 \u2265 16).\n\nThis is now a standard sphere covering problem, where we need to cover the unit sphere with spherical caps of angular radii \u03b8_i, and we need to prove that the sum of the squares of the angular radii is at least 4.\n\nIn sphere covering, the minimal sum of areas (or a similar measure) required to cover the