 cap of angular radius \\(\\theta_i\\) on the unit sphere. To cover all of space, every direction must lie within at least one cone's cap.\n\n**Step 2: Total Solid Angle Requirement**  \nThe total solid angle of the unit sphere is \\(4\\pi\\). The solid angle of a cap with angular radius \\(\\theta_i\\) is \\(2\\pi(1 - \\cos\\theta_i)\\). Since the union of caps must cover the sphere:  \n\\[\n\\sum_{i=1}^n 2\\pi(1 - \\cos\\theta_i) \\geq 4\\pi \\implies \\sum_{i=1}^n (1 - \\cos\\theta_i) \\geq 2.\n\\]\n\n**Step 3: Apply Inequality \\(1 - \\cos\\theta \\leq \\theta^2/2\\)**  \nFor \\(\\theta \\geq 0\\), the inequality \\(1 - \\cos\\theta \\leq \\theta^2/2\\) holds. Substituting:  \n\\[\n\\sum_{i=1}^n \\frac{\\theta_i^2}{2} \\geq 2 \\implies \\sum_{i=1}^n \\theta_i^2 \\geq 4.\n\\]\n\n**Step 4: Convert to Apex Angles**  \nSince \\(\\theta_i = \\varphi_i/2\\), substitute:  \n\\[\n\\sum_{i=1}^n \\left(\\frac{\\varphi_i}{2}\\right)^2 \\geq 4 \\implies \\sum_{i=1}^n \\varphi_i^2 \\geq 16.\n\\]\n\n**Conclusion**  \nTherefore, any set of cones covering the entire space must satisfy \\(\\varphi_1^2 + \\ldots + \\varphi_n^2 \\geq 16\\).\n\n\\[\n\\boxed{\\varphi_{1}^{2}+\\ldots+\\varphi_{n}^{2} \\geqslant 16}\n\\]", "ground_truth_solution": "\n1. We start by considering a space that is covered by a finite set of infinite circular cones with vertex angles $\\varphi_1, \\varphi_2, \\ldots, \\varphi_n$. The goal is to show that the sum of the squares of these angles is at least 16, i.e.,\n\n$$\n\\varphi_1^2 + \\varphi_2^2 + \\ldots + \\varphi_n^2 \\geq 16.\n$$\n\n2. To approach this, consider enclosing the vertices of all the cones within a sphere of radius \\(R\\) whose center is denoted \\(O\\). If we consider the ratio \\(R/r \\rightarrow \\infty\\), where \\(r\\) is a fixed radius, the portion of the sphere's surface included within the cones tends towards the total surface area of the sphere.\n\n3. Now, consider the surface area of the sphere: the total surface area of a sphere of radius \\(R\\) is given by\n\n$$\nS_{\\text{sphere}} = 4\\pi R^2.\n$$\n\n4. As \\(R \\rightarrow \\infty\\), the fraction of the sphere's surface that the cones with vertex angles \\(\\varphi_i\\) enclose also tends to cover the total surface area. For each cone, it is known from geometric considerations (specifically by solving Problem 4.50) that the solid angle of a cone with vertex angle \\(\\varphi\\) is:\n\n$$\n\\Omega_{\\text{cone}} = 4 \\pi \\sin^2\\left(\\frac{\\varphi}{4}\\right).\n$$\n\n5. Summing the solid angles of all $n$ cones we have:\n\n$$\n\\sum_{i=1}^n \\Omega_{\\text{cone}_i} = 4 \\pi \\sum_{i=1}^n \\sin^2\\left(\\frac{\\varphi_i}{4}\\right).\n$$\n\n6. Since the cones cover the surface of the sphere, their total solid angle must be at least the solid angle of the whole sphere, that is:\n\n$$\n4 \\pi \\sum_{i=1}^n \\sin^2\\left(\\frac{\\varphi_i}{4}\\right) \\geq 4\\pi.\n$$\n\nDividing both sides by \\(4\\pi\\) gives:\n\n$$\n\\sum_{i=1}^n \\sin^2\\left(\\frac{\\varphi_i}{4}\\right) \\geq 1.\n$$\n\n7. To bridge this with the desired inequality, recall that \\(\\sin x \\leq x\\) for \\(0 \\leq x \\leq \\frac{\\pi}{2}\\). Therefore,\n\n$$\n\\sin\\left(\\frac{\\varphi_i}{4}\\right) \\leq \\frac{\\varphi_i}{4}.\n$$\n\n8. Hence,\n\n$$\n\\sin^2\\left(\\frac{\\varphi_i}{4}\\right) \\leq \\left(\\frac{\\varphi_i}{4}\\right)^2.\n$$\n\n9. Therefore, we have:\n\n$$\n\\sum_{i=1}^n \\sin^2\\left(\\frac{\\varphi_i}{4}\\right) \\leq \\sum_{i=1}^n \\left(\\frac{\\varphi_i}{4}\\right)^2 = \\frac{1}{16} \\sum_{i=1}^n \\varphi_i^2.\n$$\n\n10. Combining the inequalities, we get:\n\n$$\n1 \\leq \\sum_{i=1}^n \\sin^2\\left(\\frac{\\varphi_i}{4}\\right) \\leq \\frac{1}{16} \\sum_{i=1}^n \\varphi_i^2,\n$$\n\nwhich implies:\n\n$$\n16 \\leq \\sum_{i=1}^n \\varphi_i^2.\n$$\n\nConclusion:\n\n\\[\n\\boxed{\\varphi_1^2 + \\varphi_2^2 + \\ldots + \\varphi_n^2 \\geq 16.}\n\\]", "domain": "math", "source": "numina_math", "test_cases": null, "starter_code": null}, {"problem": 