quaintanceships between them. We claim that the second company can guarantee itself three geniuses if the following relations hold:\n\n\\[\n\\begin{array}{llll}\nk_{0,0}=13, & k_{0,1}=12, & k_{0,2}=11, & k_{0,3}=10, \\\\\nk_{1,0}=12, & k_{1,1}=11, & k_{1,2}=10, & k_{1,3}=13, \\\\\nk_{2,0}=11, & k_{2,1}=10, & k_{2,2}=13, & k_{2,3}=12, \\\\\nk_{3,0}=10, & k_{3,1}=13, & k_{3,2}=12, & k_{3,3}=11.\n\\end{array}\n\\]\n\nWe'll call the distance from the company to the programmer the minimum number of moves required by the company at the given stage to take this programmer into employment. Let's describe the strategy of the second company in three possible cases.\n\n1. **Case 1: The first company initially chooses one of the famous programmers.** Then the second company should choose a famous programmer so that 3 out of 4 geniuses are closer to the second company than to the first. For example, if the first company takes \\( F_0 \\), the second company should take \\( F_1 \\) to be closer to \\( G_0, G_1 \\), and \\( G_2 \\) than the first company.\n\n2. **Case 2: The first company initially takes a genius (say \\( G_3 \\)).** Then the second company takes any famous programmer.\n\n3. **Case 3: The first company initially takes a programmer from the chain connecting some genius (say \\( G_3 \\)) and some famous programmer \\( F_i \\).** Then the second company should take programmer \\( F_i \\).\n\nIn the notations of all three described cases, the second company can guarantee itself geniuses \\( G_0, G_1, G_2 \\) by adhering to the following tactics. If on a certain step the first company takes a programmer from some chain leading to a genius \\( G_j \\) where \\( j \\leq 2 \\), the second company shortens the distance to the genius \\( G_j \\) by choosing the next programmer from the chain leading to him. Otherwise, the second company shortens the distance to the farthest of the three geniuses \\( G_0, G_1, G_2 \\) (if there are 2 or all 3, it chooses from them arbitrarily).\n\nIt isn't difficult to see that after each move of the second company, geniuses \\( G_0, G_1, G_2 \\) will be closer to the second company than to the first, which means that the first company will not be able to capture them.\n\n### Conclusion:\n\nYes, the acquaintanceship structure can be set up such that the second company, entering the game second, can always hire at least 3 geniuses, regardless of the actions of the first company.\n\nTherefore, the answer is:\n\n\\[\n\\boxed{\\text{Yes}}\n\\]\n\nNow, let's address the additional reference solution regarding another problem.\n\nLet's denote \\( N \\neq 2^k \\). Considering the scenario where we rotate the circle, and Ali-Baba cannot determine which barrels have been flipped.\n\n#### A step-by-step explanation:\n\n1. **When \\( N \\) is odd:**\n   - Initially, place zeros and ones on the circle such that Ali-Baba cannot win by his first move (we ensure that after his move, both zeros and ones remain on the circle).\n\n2. **If Ali-Baba chooses to flip \\( k \\) barrels:**\n   - He wins only if these \\( k \\) positions coincide with either all the zeros or all the ones. However, since the number of zeros is not equal to the number of ones (the total is odd), at least one type of digit will not be \\( k \\). Thus, rotating to bring a different digit into one chosen position, we prevent Ali-Baba from winning the next move.\n\n3. **When \\( N \\) is even:**\n   - If \\( N \\) has an odd divisor \\( m \\), we reduce the problem to the odd case:\n     - Mark \\( m \\) equally spaced positions on the large circle and ignore the rest.\n     - Follow the previously described strategy to prevent Ali-Baba from equalizing all digits at the marked positions.\n\nIf \\( N \\) is a power of 2 (\\( N = 2^k \\)), we can construct a winning algorithm inductively:\n- **Base case \\( k=1 \\) is trivial.**\n- **Inductive step:**\n  - Suppose we have an algorithm \\( A_m \\) for \\( m \\) barrels and construct \\( A_{2m} \\).\n\n#### Applying the \\( A_{2m} \\) algorithm:\n1. **Equal Barrels:**\n   - If each pair of barrels has equal digits, apply \\( A_m \\) for corresponding pairs.\n\n2. **Summing Even/Odd:**\n   - If sums have the same parity, use \\( A_m \\) and adjust pairs.\n   - If sums are odd, flip digits in all selected \\( m \\) pairs.\n\n3. **General Strategy:**\n   - Change parities until sums match, then apply the consistent flipping strategy.\n\n### Conclusion\nFor Ali-Baba to open the cave eventually:\n\n\\[\n\\boxed{When \\quad N = 2^k, \\quad k = 0,1,2, \\ldots}\n\\]", "domain": "math", "source": "numina_math", "test_cases": null, "starter_code": null}, {"problem": "One worker makes 5 more parts in two hours than another worker. Therefore, it takes him 2 hours less to produce 100 parts. How much time does each worker spend producing 100 parts?", "deepseek_reasoning": "Okay, let's try to figure out this problem step by step. So, we have two workers. The first worker makes 5 more parts in two hours than the second worker. And because of that, he takes 2 hours less to produce 100 parts. We need to find out how much time each worker spends to produce 100 parts. Hmm, let's break this down.\n\nFirst, let me define some variables. Let's say the slower worker (the second one) makes x parts in two hours. Then, the faster worker (the first one) makes x + 5 parts in two hours. Wait, the problem says \"one worker makes 5 more parts in two hours than another worker.\" So, if the second worker makes x parts in two hours, the first makes x + 5 in the same time. That makes sense.\n\nNow, I need to relate their rates to the time it takes to make 100 parts. Let's think about their rates. Rate is parts per hour. So, for the second worker, if he makes x parts in 2 hours, his rate is x/2 parts per hour. Similarly, the first worker's rate is (x + 5)/2 parts per hour.\n\nTo produce 100 parts, time is equal to the number of parts divided by the rate. So, for the second worker, the time to make 100 parts would be 100 divided by (x/2). Which is 100 * (2/x) = 200/x hours. Similarly, the first worker would take 100 divided by [(x + 5)/2] = 200/(x + 5) hours.\n\nAccording to the problem, the first worker takes 2 hours less than the second. So, the time taken by the second worker minus the time taken by the first worker equals 2 hours. That gives the equation:\n\n200/x - 200/(x + 5) = 2\n\nOkay, so now we have an equation in terms of x. Let's solve this equation for x. Let's write that down:\n\n200/x - 200/(x + 5) = 2\n\nTo solve this, I can first factor out 200:\n\n200[1/x - 1/(x + 5)] = 2\n\nThen divide both sides by 200:\n\n[1/x - 1/(x + 5)] = 2/200 = 1/100\n\nNow, let's compute the left side:\n\n1/x - 1/(x + 5) = [ (x + 5) - x ] / [x(x + 5)] = 5 / [x(x + 5)]\n\nSo, 5 / [x(x + 5)] = 1/100\n\nCross-multiplying:\n\n5 * 100 = x(x + 5)\n\nSo, 500 = x\u00b2 + 5x\n\nBring all terms to one side:\n\nx\u00b2 + 5x - 500 = 0\n\nNow, solve this quadratic equation. Let's use the quadratic formula:\n\nx = [-b \u00b1 \u221a(b\u00b2 - 4ac)] / 2a\n\nHere, a = 1, b = 5, c = -500\n\nDiscriminant: b\u00b2 - 4ac = 25 + 2000 = 2025\n\n\u221a2025 = 45\n\nSo, x = [-5 \u00b1 45]/2\n\nWe can discard the negative solution because parts produced can't be negative. So,\n\nx = (-5 + 45)/2 = 40/2 = 20\n\nSo, the second worker makes x = 20 parts in two hours, which means his rate is 10 parts per hour. The first worker makes x + 5 = 25 parts in two hours, so his rate is 12.5 parts per hour.\n\nNow, let's check if this makes sense. The time for the second worker to make 100 parts is 100 / 10 = 10 hours. The first worker would take 100 / 12.5 = 8 hours. The difference is 10 - 8 = 2 hours, which matches the problem statement. So that seems correct.\n\nTherefore, the time taken by each worker is 10 hours for the slower worker and 8 hours for the faster worker.\n\nWait, but let me double-check my equations. Maybe I made a mistake in the setup.\n\nWait, the problem says \"one worker makes 5 more parts in two hours than another worker.\" So, if the second worker makes 20 parts in two hours, the first makes 25. That's 5 more, correct. Then their rates are 10 and 12.5 parts per hour. Then 100 parts would take 10 and 8 hours, which is a difference of 2 hours. That's all correct. So, seems like the answer is 8 hours and 10 hours.\n\nBut let me think again. Let me approach this using different variables to cross-validate.\n\nSuppose we let the time taken by the first worker to produce 100 parts be t hours. Then the second worker takes t + 2 hours.\n\nSo, the rate of the first worker is 100/t parts per hour, and the rate of the second is 100/(t + 2) parts per hour.\n\nThe problem states that the first worker makes 5 more parts in two hours. So, in two hours, the first worker makes 2*(100/t) = 200/t parts, and the second worker makes 2*(100/(t + 2)) = 200/(t + 2) parts. The difference is 5 parts:\n\n200/t - 200/(t + 2) = 5\n\nLet me solve this equation. Let's see:\n\n200[1/t - 1/(t + 2)] = 5\n\n1/t - 1/(t + 2) = 5/200 = 1/40\n\nCombine the fractions:\n\n[(t + 2) - t] / [t(t + 2)] = 1/40\n\n2 / [t(t + 2)] = 1/40\n\nMultiply both sides by t(t + 2):\n\n2 = [t(t + 2)]/40\n\nMultiply both sides by 40:\n\n80 = t(t + 2)\n\nSo, t\u00b2 + 2t - 80 = 0\n\nSolving this quadratic equation:\n\nt = [-2 \u00b1 \u221a(4 + 320)] / 2 = [-2 \u00b1 \u221a324]/2 = [-2 \u00b1 18]/2\n\nTaking the positive solution:\n\nt = (-2 + 18)/2 = 16/2 = 8\n\nSo, t = 8 hours for the first worker, and t + 2 = 10 hours for the second worker. Same result as before. So, this confirms that the answer is 8 and 10 hours.\n\nTherefore, the time each worker spends producing 100 parts is 8 hours for the faster worker and 10 hours for the slower worker.\n\nBut let me just check once again. If the first worker takes 8 hours for 100 parts, his rate is 100/8 = 12.5 parts per hour. In two hours, he makes 25 parts. The second worker takes 10 hours, rate is 100/10 = 10 parts per hour. In two hours, he makes 20 parts. The difference is indeed 25 - 20 = 5 parts. Perfect, matches the problem statement.\n\nSo, both approaches lead to the same answer, so it's correct. The first worker takes 8 hours, the second takes 10 hours.", "deepseek_solution": "Let the number of parts the second worker produces in two hours be \\( x \\). The first worker then produces \\( x + 5 \\) parts in the same time. Their hourly rates are \\( \\frac{x}{2} \\) and \\( \\frac{x + 5}{2} \\) parts per hour, respectively. The time to produce 100 parts for each worker is:\n\n- Second worker: \\( \\frac{100}{\\frac{x}{2}} = \\frac{200}{x} \\) hours\n- First worker: \\( \\frac{100}{\\frac{x + 5}{2}} = \\frac{200}{x + 5} \\) hours\n\nGiven the time difference of 2 hours:\n\\[\n\\frac{200}{x} - \\frac{200}{x + 5} = 2\n\\]\n\nSimplifying:\n\\[\n200 \\left( \\frac{1}{x} - \\frac{1}{x + 5} \\right) = 2 \\implies \\frac{5}{x(x + 5)} = \\frac{1}{100} \\implies x(x + 5) = 500\n\\]\nSolving the quadratic \\( x^2 + 5x - 500 = 0 \\):\n\\[\nx = \\frac{-5 \\pm \\sqrt{25 + 2000}}{2} = \\frac{-5 \\pm 45}{2}\n\\]\nTaking the positive root: \\( x = 20 \\). Thus, the second worker produces 20 parts in 2 hours (10 parts/hour), and the first worker produces 25 parts in 2 hours (12.5 parts/hour). \n\nThe time to produce 100 parts:\n- Second worker: \\( \\frac{100}{10} = 10 \\) hours  \n- First worker: \\( \\frac{100}{12.5} = 8 \\) hours  \n\n**Answer:**  \nThe first worker takes \\(\\boxed{8}\\) hours and the second worker takes \\(\\boxed{10}\\) hours.", "ground_truth_solution": "\n1. Let's denote by \\(x\\) the number of hours the second worker takes to make 100 parts. This means that the first worker takes \\(x - 2\\) hours to make 100 parts.\n\n2. The rate (parts per hour) at which the second worker works is given by:\n   \\[\n   \\frac{100 \\text{ parts}}{x \\text{ hours}}\n   \\]\n   Similarly, the rate at which the first worker works is:\n   \\[\n   \\frac{100 \\text{ parts}}{x - 2 \\text{ hours}}\n   \\]\n\n3. We are also given that the first worker can make 5 more parts in 2 hours compared to the second worker. This leads us to the following equation:\n   \\[\n   \\frac{100}{x-2} - \\frac{100}{x} = \\frac{5}{2}\n   \\]\n\n4. To simplify this equation, first find a common denominator for the fractions on the left side:\n   \\[\n   \\frac{100x - 100(x - 2)}{(x-2)x} = \\frac{5}{2}\n   \\]\n   Simplifying the numerator on the left side:\n   \\[\n   \\frac{100x - 100x + 200}{x(x - 2)} = \\frac{5}{2}\n   \\]\n   Which further simplifies to:\n   \\[\n   \\frac{200}{x(x - 2)} = \\frac{5}{2}\n   \\]\n\n5. Cross-multiplying to solve for \\(x\\), we get:\n   \\[\n   200 \\cdot 2 = 5x(x - 2)\n   \\]\n   Simplifying:\n   \\[\n   400 = 5x^2 - 10x\n   \\]\n\n6. Divide the entire equation by 5 to simplify:\n   \\[\n   80 = x^2 - 2x\n   \\]\n   Rewriting it in a standard quadratic equation form:\n   \\[\n   x^2 - 2x - 80 = 0\n   \\]\n\n7. Solve the quadratic equation using the quadratic formula:\n   \\[\n   x = \\frac{-b \\pm \\sqrt{b^2 - 4ac}}{2a}\n   \\]\n   Here, \\(a = 1\\), \\(b = -2\\), and \\(c = -80\\). So, we have:\n   \\[\n   x = \\frac{2 \\pm \\sqrt{4 + 320}}{2}\n   \\]\n   Simplifying inside the square root:\n   \\[\n   x = \\frac{2 \\pm \\sqrt{324}}{2}\n   \\]\n   Taking the square root of 324:\n   \\[\n   x = \\frac{2 \\pm 18}{2}\n   \\]\n\n8. This gives us two possible solutions:\n   \\[\n   x = \\frac{2 + 18}{2} = 10 \\quad \\text{or} \\quad x = \\frac{2 - 18}{2} = -8\n   \\]\n   Since \\(x\\) represents time, which cannot be negative, we take:\n   \\[\n   x = 10\n   \\]\n\n9. Therefore, the second worker takes \\(10\\) hours to make 100 parts and the first worker takes \\(x - 2 = 10 - 2 = 8\\) hours to make the same 100 parts.\n\n### Conclusion:\n\\[ \\boxed{8 \\text{ \u0447\u0430\u0441\u043e\u0432 \u0438 } 10 \\text{ \u0447\u0430\u0441\u043e\u0432}} \\]", "domain": "math", "source": "numina_math", "test_cases": null, "starter_code": null}, {"problem": "Let \\( S \\) be the sum of the digits of the number \\( 11^{2017} \\). Find the remainder when \\( S \\) is divided by 9.", "deepseek_reasoning": "Okay, so I need to find the remainder when the sum of the digits of 11^2017 is divided by 9. Hmm, let's start by understanding the problem step by step. \n\nFirst, I know that when dealing with remainders and digit sums, modular arithmetic, especially modulo 9, might be useful because of the divisibility rule for 9. The rule says that a number is divisible by 9 if the sum of its digits is divisible by 9. Also, the remainder when a number is divided by 9 is the same as the remainder when the sum of its digits is divided by 9. So, essentially, the problem is asking for 11^2017 mod 9. Wait, is that right? Let me confirm.\n\nYes, because if S is the sum of the digits of 11^2017, then S \u2261 11^2017 mod 9. So, instead of calculating 11^2017, summing its digits (which would be a huge number), and then taking mod 9, I can directly compute 11^2017 mod 9. That simplifies things a lot because calculating 11^2017 directly is impractical.\n\nSo, let's compute 11 mod 9 first. 11 divided by 9 is 1 with a remainder of 2. Therefore, 11 \u2261 2 mod 9. Therefore, 11^2017 \u2261 2^2017 mod 9. Now the problem reduces to finding 2^2017 mod 9.\n\nTo compute 2^2017 mod 9, I can look for a pattern in the powers of 2 modulo 9. Let's list them out:\n\n2^1 \u2261 2 mod 9\n\n2^2 \u2261 4 mod 9\n\n2^3 \u2261 8 mod 9\n\n2^4 \u2261 16 \u2261 7 mod 9\n\n2^5 \u2261 14 \u2261 5 mod 9\n\n2^6 \u2261 10 \u2261 1 mod 9\n\n2^7 \u2261 2 mod 9\n\nHmm, so there's a cycle every 6 exponents. The powers of 2 modulo 9 repeat every 6 steps. So, the period is 6. Therefore, 2^(6k) \u2261 1 mod 9 for any integer k.\n\nNow, 2017 divided by 6. Let's compute that. 6*336=2016, so 2017=6*336 +1. So, 2^2017 \u2261 2^(6*336 +1) \u2261 (2^6)^336 * 2^1 \u2261 1^336 * 2 \u2261 2 mod 9.\n\nTherefore, 2^2017 \u2261 2 mod 9. Hence, 11^2017 \u2261 2 mod 9. Therefore, the sum of the digits S \u2261 2 mod 9. So, the remainder when S is divided by 9 is 2.\n\nWait, let me verify this again to make sure I didn't make a mistake. \n\nFirst, 11 mod 9 is indeed 2. Then, 2^2017 mod 9. The cycle length is 6, correct? Because 2^6 \u2261 1 mod 9. So, exponents cycle every 6. Then, 2017 divided by 6 is 336 with a remainder of 1. So, 2^(6*336 +1) = (2^6)^336 * 2^1 \u2261 1^336 * 2 \u2261 2 mod 9. That seems right.\n\nTherefore, the remainder is 2. So, the answer should be 2. Let me check with a smaller exponent to see if this holds. For example, take 11^1 = 11. Sum of digits is 1 + 1 = 2. 2 mod 9 is 2. Which matches 11^1 mod 9 = 2. \n\nAnother example: 11^2 = 121. Sum of digits is 1 + 2 + 1 = 4. 121 mod 9: 121 divided by 9 is 13*9=117, remainder 4. Which matches. \n\n11^3 = 1331. Sum of digits is 1+3+3+1=8. 1331 mod 9: 1331 - 9*147=1331-1323=8. Correct. \n\n11^4=14641. Sum of digits is 1+4+6+4+1=16. 16 mod 9 is 7. 14641 mod 9: Let's compute 14641/9. 9*1627=14643. 14641 -14643= -2, but we take positive remainder, so 9 -2 =7. Correct. \n\n11^5=161051. Sum of digits:1+6+1+0+5+1=14. 14 mod9=5. 161051 mod9: 161051/9. Let's compute 9*17894=161046, remainder 5. Correct.\n\n11^6=1771561. Sum of digits:1+7+7+1+5+6+1=22. 22 mod9=4. But according to our previous cycle, 2^6=64 mod9=1, so 11^6 mod9=1. Wait, but 1771561 mod9: Let's compute 1+7+7+1+5+6+1=22. 22 mod9=4. Wait, but according to the previous logic, 11^6 \u22611 mod9. There's a discrepancy here. What's wrong?\n\nWait a second, 11^6 mod9 should be 1, as 2^6=64\u22611 mod9. But calculating 11^6's digit sum mod9 gives 22 mod9=4, which is different. That contradicts. That means my earlier reasoning is flawed. Hmm, so where did I go wrong?\n\nWait, the problem is that S \u2261 number mod9, but S is the sum of digits. So, the sum of digits of 11^6 is 22, which is 22 mod9=4. But 11^6 mod9 is 1. So, that's a contradiction. But that can't be. Wait, what's 11^6?\n\nCompute 11^1=11, 11^2=121, 11^3=1331, 11^4=14641, 11^5=161051, 11^6=1771561. The sum of digits for 11^6 is 1+7+7+1+5+6+1=22. 22 mod9=4. But 11^6 mod9= (11 mod9)^6=2^6=64\u22611 mod9. But 1\u22604. Therefore, there's a contradiction here. Which means my initial assumption that S \u2261 11^2017 mod9 is wrong? But that conflicts with the divisibility rule for 9.\n\nWait, no, the divisibility rule for 9 says that a number is congruent to the sum of its digits mod9. So, 11^6 \u2261 sum of its digits mod9. But according to my calculation, 11^6 mod9=1, but sum of digits is 22, which is 4 mod9. 1\u22614 mod9? No, that's not possible. Therefore, either my calculation of 11^6 is wrong, or the sum of digits is wrong. Let me check 11^6 again.\n\nCalculating 11^6 step by step:\n\n11^1 = 11\n\n11^2 = 11*11 = 121\n\n11^3 = 121*11 = 1331\n\n11^4 = 1331*11 = 14641\n\n11^5 = 14641*11 = 161,051\n\n11^6 = 161,051*11 = 1,771,561\n\nSum of digits: 1 + 7 + 7 + 1 + 5 + 6 + 1 = 22. Yes, 22. 22 mod9 is 4. But 11^6 mod9: 11 mod9=2, so 2^6=64. 64 mod9: 9*7=63, 64-63=1. So 64\u22611 mod9. So 11^6\u22611 mod9. But 1\u22611 mod9, and 22\u22614 mod9. So 1\u22614 mod9? That's not possible. Therefore, there's a contradiction here, which means my initial reasoning was wrong.\n\nWait, this is a problem. Therefore, my assumption that S \u2261 11^n mod9 is incorrect? But that contradicts the divisibility rule. Wait, maybe I miscalculated the sum of digits. Let me check 11^6 again.\n\n11^6 is 1,771,561. Let's verify the digits: 1, 7, 7, 1, 5, 6, 1. So adding them up: 1+7=8, 8+7=15, 15+1=16, 16+5=21, 21+6=27, 27+1=28. Wait, wait, that's 28, not 22. Wait, did I miscalculate earlier? Let's see:\n\n1 (first digit) +7 (second) =8, then next digit 7: 8+7=15, then 1: 15+1=16, then 5: 16+5=21, then 6:21+6=27, then 1:27+1=28. So the sum is 28. 28 mod9: 28 divided by9 is3*9=27, remainder1. So 28\u22611 mod9. Which matches 11^6\u22611 mod9. Wait, so earlier I thought the sum was22, but actually it's28. So that resolves the contradiction. So my mistake was in the sum of digits for 11^6. \n\nSo in my earlier calculation, I said 11^6 is 1,771,561, and sum of digits 1+7+7+1+5+6+1. Wait, 1 (million place) +7 (hundred thousands) +7 (ten thousands) +1 (thousands) +5 (hundreds) +6 (tens) +1 (units). That's correct. 1+7+7+1+5+6+1=28. So why did I write 22 earlier? That was a miscalculation. So the sum is28, which is1 mod9. So that matches with 11^6\u22611 mod9. So my initial calculation was wrong because I added the digits incorrectly. So, no problem with the rule.\n\nTherefore, returning to the original problem. The key idea is correct: the sum of digits of 11^2017 \u226111^2017 mod9\u22612^2017 mod9. Then, since 2^6\u22611 mod9, 2^2017=2^(6*336 +1)= (2^6)^336 *2^1\u22611^336 *2\u22612 mod9. So the remainder is2. \n\nLet me confirm again with another exponent where the sum of digits is manageable. For example, 11^1=11, sum digits 1+1=2\u22612 mod9. 11 mod9=2. Correct. 11^2=121, sum digits1+2+1=4\u22614 mod9. 121 mod9=4. Correct. 11^3=1331, sum digits1+3+3+1=8\u22618 mod9. 1331 mod9=8. Correct. 11^4=14641, sum digits1+4+6+4+1=16\u22617 mod9. 14641 mod9: 14641/9=1627*9=14643, 14641-14643=-2\u22617 mod9. Correct. 11^5=161051, sum digits1+6+1+0+5+1=14\u22615 mod9. 161051 mod9: 161051/9=17894*9=161046, remainder5. Correct. 11^6=1771561, sum digits1+7+7+1+5+6+1=28\u22611 mod9. 1771561 mod9=1. Correct. So the pattern holds. \n\nTherefore, the conclusion that S \u22612 mod9 is correct. Therefore, the remainder is2. \n\nJust to be thorough, let's check 11^7. 11^7=1771561*11=19487171. Sum digits1+9+4+8+7+1+7+1=30. 30 mod9=3. 11^7 mod9=2^7\u22612 mod9. Wait, 2^7=128\u2261128-14*9=128-126=2 mod9. Yes, so 30 mod9=3. Wait, but 2^7\u22612 mod9, but sum is3. Wait, that contradicts again. Wait, no, 11^7 mod9=2^7\u22612 mod9. But sum of digits=30\u22613 mod9. 2\u22613 mod9? No, that's not. Therefore, there's a problem again. What's wrong here?\n\nWait, let's compute 11^7 again. 11^6=1771561. 1771561*11=1771561*10 +1771561=17715610 +1771561=19487171. Sum of digits:1+9+4+8+7+1+7+1=1+9=10, 10+4=14,14+8=22,22+7=29,29+1=30,30+7=37,37+1=38. Wait, hold on, I think I added incorrectly earlier. Let's recount:\n\n1 (first digit) +9=10, next digit4: 10+4=14, next digit8:14+8=22, next digit7:22+7=29, next digit1:29+1=30, next digit7:30+7=37, next digit1:37+1=38. So the sum of digits is38. 38 mod9: 38-36=2, so 38\u22612 mod9. Which matches 11^7 mod9=2. So my mistake was again in adding the digits. Originally, I thought it was30, but actually it's38. So, that's why. So, no contradiction. Therefore, the sum of digits of 11^7 is38\u22612 mod9, which is equal to11^7 mod9=2. \n\nTherefore, my earlier confusion was due to incorrect digit sums. So, the rule holds: sum of digits \u2261 number mod9. Therefore, the original reasoning is correct. Therefore, the answer is2.\n\n**Final Answer**\n\\boxed{2}", "deepseek_solution": "To find the remainder when the sum of the digits of \\( 11^{2017} \\) is divided by 9, we use the property that a number is congruent to the sum of its digits modulo 9. \n\nFirst, we compute \\( 11 \\mod 9 \\):\n\\[ 11 \\equiv 2 \\mod 9 \\]\n\nThus, we need to find \\( 2^{2017} \\mod 9 \\). We observe the powers of 2 modulo 9:\n\\[\n\\begin{align*}\n2^1 &\\equiv 2 \\mod 9, \\\\\n2^2 &\\equiv 4 \\mod 9, \\\\\n2^3 &\\equiv 8 \\mod 9, \\\\\n2^4 &\\equiv 16 \\equiv 7 \\mod 9, \\\\\n2^5 &\\equiv 14 \\equiv 5 \\mod 9, \\\\\n2^6 &\\equiv 64 \\equiv 1 \\mod 9.\n\\end{align*}\n\\]\nThe powers of 2 modulo 9 repeat every 6 steps. Therefore, \\( 2^{2017} \\) can be simplified by noting the remainder of 2017 when divided by 6:\n\\[ 2017 = 6 \\times 336 + 1 \\]\nThus,\n\\[ 2^{2017} \\equiv 2^{6 \\times 336 + 1} \\equiv (2^6)^{336} \\times 2^1 \\equiv 1^{336} \\times 2 \\equiv 2 \\mod 9. \\]\n\nTherefore, the remainder when the sum of the digits of \\( 11^{2017} \\) is divided by 9 is \\(\\boxed{2}\\).", "ground_truth_solution": "\n1. **Understand the Problem and Key Insight**:\n   The task is to find the remainder when the sum of the digits of \\(11^{2017}\\) is divided by 9.\n\n2. **Sum of Digits Relationship with Modulus 9**:\n   Recall that the sum of the digits of a number gives the same remainder when divided by 9 as the number itself. This follows from the properties of congruences:\n   \\[\n   \\text{If } n = a_0 + a_1 \\cdot 10 + a_2 \\cdot 10^2 + \\ldots + a_k \\cdot 10^k \\text{, then } n \\equiv a_0 + a_1 + a_2 + \\ldots + a_k \\pmod{9}\n   \\]\n\n3. **Expression Simplification**:\n   We need to compute \\(11^{2017} \\mod 9\\).\n\n4. **Apply Modular Arithmetic**:\n   Start by reducing base 11 modulo 9:\n   \\[\n   11 \\equiv 2 \\pmod{9}\n   \\]\n\n5. **Exponentiation in Modular Arithmetic**:\n   Now, compute the equivalent modulo for the exponent:\n   \\[\n   11^{2017} \\equiv 2^{2017} \\pmod{9}\n   \\]\n\n6. **Pattern Recognition or Simplification in Powers**:\n   Notice the pattern in powers of 2 modulo 9:\n   \\[\n   2^1 \\equiv 2 \\pmod{9}\n   \\]\n   \\[\n   2^2 \\equiv 4 \\pmod{9}\n   \\]\n   \\[\n   2^3 \\equiv 8 \\pmod{9}\n   \\]\n   \\[\n   2^4 \\equiv 16 \\equiv 7 \\pmod{9}\n   \\]\n   \\[\n   2^5 \\equiv 2 \\cdot 7 = 14 \\equiv 5 \\pmod{9}\n   \\]\n   \\[\n   2^6 \\equiv 2 \\cdot 5 = 10 \\equiv 1 \\pmod{9}\n   \\]\n\n   Therefore, \\(2^6 \\equiv 1 \\pmod{9}\\).\n\n7. **Calculate Remainder Using Cyclic Nature**:\n   From the above, \\(2^6 \\equiv 1 \\pmod{9}\\), so \\(2^{6k} \\equiv 1 \\pmod{9}\\) for any integer \\(k\\). For \\(2017\\):\n   \\[\n   2017 = 6 \\cdot 336 + 1\n   \\]\n   Giving:\n   \\[\n   2^{2017} = 2^{6 \\cdot 336 + 1} = (2^6)^{336} \\cdot 2^1 \\equiv 1^{336} \\cdot 2 \\equiv 2 \\pmod{9}\n   \\]\n\n8. **Conclusion**:\n   Hence, the remainder when the sum of the digits of \\(11^{2017}\\) is divided by 9 is:\n   \\[\n   \\boxed{2}\n   \\]", "domain": "math", "source": "numina_math", "test_cases": null, "starter_code": null}, {"problem": "In triangle \\( \\triangle ABC \\), the sides opposite angles \\( A \\), \\( B \\), and \\( C \\) are \\( a \\), \\( b \\), and \\( c \\), respectively. The following conditions are given:\n\\[ (2a - c) \\cos B = b \\cos C \\]\n\\[ \\sin^2 A = \\sin^2 B + \\sin^2 C - \\lambda \\sin B \\sin C \\quad (\\lambda \\in \\mathbf{R}) \\]\n\n1. Find the measure of angle \\( B \\).\n2. If \\( \\lambda = \\sqrt{3} \\), determine the shape of triangle \\( \\triangle ABC \\).\n3. If triangle \\( \\triangle ABC \\) is an obtuse triangle, determine the range of values for the real number \\( \\lambda \\).", "deepseek_reasoning": "Alright, let's tackle this triangle problem step by step. There are three parts to solve, and each seems to build on the previous one. Let me start by understanding the given conditions and then proceed to each question.\n\nFirst, the triangle ABC has sides a, b, c opposite angles A, B, C respectively. The two given equations are:\n\n1. (2a - c) cos B = b cos C\n2. sin\u00b2A = sin\u00b2B + sin\u00b2C - \u03bb sin B sin C, where \u03bb is a real number.\n\nWe need to find the measure of angle B, determine the shape of the triangle when \u03bb = \u221a3, and find the range of \u03bb if the triangle is obtuse.\n\nStarting with part 1: Find angle B.\n\nThe first equation given is (2a - c) cos B = b cos C. Let me recall the Law of Cosines and Sines because those might be helpful here.\n\nLaw of Cosines states that for any triangle,\n\na\u00b2 = b\u00b2 + c\u00b2 - 2bc cos A\nSimilarly for other angles.\n\nLaw of Sines states that a/sin A = b/sin B = c/sin C = 2R, where R is the circumradius.\n\nMaybe I can express cos B and cos C using the Law of Cosines, or perhaps express sides in terms of angles using the Law of Sines. Let me try substituting the Law of Sines into the first equation.\n\nFrom Law of Sines:\n\na = 2R sin A\nb = 2R sin B\nc = 2R sin C\n\nSo, substituting these into the first equation:\n\n(2*(2R sin A) - 2R sin C) cos B = 2R sin B cos C\n\nSimplify by factoring out 2R:\n\n2R [2 sin A - sin C] cos B = 2R sin B cos C\n\nDivide both sides by 2R (since R \u2260 0):\n\n(2 sin A - sin C) cos B = sin B cos C\n\nLet me rearrange this equation:\n\n2 sin A cos B - sin C cos B = sin B cos C\n\nBring all terms to the left side:\n\n2 sin A cos B - sin C cos B - sin B cos C = 0\n\nHmm, maybe we can combine the last two terms using a trigonometric identity. Recall that sin(B + C) = sin B cos C + cos B sin C. So, sin C cos B + sin B cos C = sin(B + C). But here we have - sin C cos B - sin B cos C = - sin(B + C). However, in a triangle, B + C = \u03c0 - A, so sin(B + C) = sin(\u03c0 - A) = sin A. Therefore, - sin(B + C) = - sin A. Let me check:\n\nWait, let's see:\n\nsin C cos B + sin B cos C = sin(B + C) = sin(\u03c0 - A) = sin A. Therefore, - (sin C cos B + sin B cos C) = - sin A. Therefore, the equation becomes:\n\n2 sin A cos B - sin A = 0\n\nFactor sin A:\n\nsin A (2 cos B - 1) = 0\n\nSo, either sin A = 0 or 2 cos B - 1 = 0.\n\nBut in a triangle, angles are between 0 and \u03c0, so sin A = 0 would imply A = 0 or \u03c0, which is impossible. Therefore, we must have 2 cos B - 1 = 0, so cos B = 1/2. Therefore, angle B is \u03c0/3 or 60 degrees.\n\nSo, part 1's answer is 60 degrees or \u03c0/3 radians. Let me check that again to make sure.\n\nWait, step-by-step:\n\nOriginal equation: (2a - c) cos B = b cos C\n\nConverted via Law of Sines to:\n\n(2 sin A - sin C) cos B = sin B cos C\n\nThen rearranged to:\n\n2 sin A cos B = sin B cos C + sin C cos B\n\nBut sin B cos C + sin C cos B = sin(B + C) = sin(\u03c0 - A) = sin A\n\nSo, 2 sin A cos B = sin A\n\nDivide both sides by sin A (assuming sin A \u2260 0, which it can't be in a triangle):\n\n2 cos B = 1 => cos B = 1/2 => B = \u03c0/3 or 60 degrees.\n\nYes, that seems correct. So angle B is 60 degrees. So part 1 answer is 60 degrees. Good.\n\nNow moving on to part 2: If \u03bb = \u221a3, determine the shape of triangle ABC.\n\nWe have the second equation given: sin\u00b2A = sin\u00b2B + sin\u00b2C - \u03bb sin B sin C.\n\nSince we know angle B is 60 degrees from part 1, maybe we can use that to find relations between angles A and C, and then determine the shape.\n\nFirst, since in a triangle, A + B + C = \u03c0, so A + C = \u03c0 - B = \u03c0 - \u03c0/3 = 2\u03c0/3. So, A + C = 120 degrees.\n\nSo, A = 120 degrees - C.\n\nGiven that, let's substitute A = 2\u03c0/3 - C into the second equation.\n\nBut first, let's write down the equation with B = \u03c0/3:\n\nsin\u00b2A = sin\u00b2(\u03c0/3) + sin\u00b2C - \u221a3 sin(\u03c0/3) sin C.\n\nCompute sin(\u03c0/3) = \u221a3/2, so sin\u00b2(\u03c0/3) = 3/4.\n\nSo:\n\nsin\u00b2A = 3/4 + sin\u00b2C - \u221a3*(\u221a3/2) sin C.\n\nCompute \u221a3*(\u221a3/2) = (3)/2.\n\nSo:\n\nsin\u00b2A = 3/4 + sin\u00b2C - (3/2) sin C.\n\nBut A = 2\u03c0/3 - C, so sin A = sin(2\u03c0/3 - C).\n\nUsing sine of a difference:\n\nsin(2\u03c0/3 - C) = sin(2\u03c0/3) cos C - cos(2\u03c0/3) sin C.\n\nCompute sin(2\u03c0/3) = \u221a3/2, cos(2\u03c0/3) = -1/2.\n\nThus:\n\nsin A = (\u221a3/2) cos C - (-1/2) sin C = (\u221a3/2) cos C + (1/2) sin C.\n\nTherefore, sin\u00b2A = [ (\u221a3/2 cos C + 1/2 sin C ) ]\u00b2\n\nExpanding:\n\n= (3/4 cos\u00b2 C + (\u221a3/2)(1/2) sin C cos C + 1/4 sin\u00b2 C )\n\n= (3/4 cos\u00b2 C + (\u221a3/4) sin 2C + 1/4 sin\u00b2 C )\n\nAlternatively, let's compute it step by step:\n\n= ( (\u221a3/2 cos C ) + (1/2 sin C ) )\u00b2\n\n= (\u221a3/2 cos C )\u00b2 + 2*(\u221a3/2 cos C)*(1/2 sin C ) + (1/2 sin C )\u00b2\n\n= (3/4 cos\u00b2 C) + ( \u221a3/2 * 1/2 * 2 sin C cos C ) + (1/4 sin\u00b2 C )\n\nWait, the cross term:\n\n2*(\u221a3/2 cos C)*(1/2 sin C ) = 2*(\u221a3/4 sin C cos C ) = \u221a3/2 sin C cos C\n\nSo, sin\u00b2A = 3/4 cos\u00b2 C + \u221a3/2 sin C cos C + 1/4 sin\u00b2 C.\n\nNow, set this equal to the right-hand side:\n\n3/4 + sin\u00b2C - (3/2) sin C\n\nTherefore:\n\n3/4 cos\u00b2 C + \u221a3/2 sin C cos C + 1/4 sin\u00b2 C = 3/4 + sin\u00b2C - (3/2) sin C\n\nLet me rearrange this equation:\n\nLeft side: 3/4 cos\u00b2 C + 1/4 sin\u00b2 C + \u221a3/2 sin C cos C\n\nRight side: 3/4 + sin\u00b2C - (3/2) sin C\n\nBring all terms to the left:\n\n3/4 cos\u00b2 C + 1/4 sin\u00b2 C + \u221a3/2 sin C cos C - 3/4 - sin\u00b2C + (3/2) sin C = 0\n\nSimplify term by term:\n\nFirst term: 3/4 cos\u00b2 C\n\nSecond term: 1/4 sin\u00b2 C\n\nThird term: \u221a3/2 sin C cos C\n\nFourth term: -3/4\n\nFifth term: - sin\u00b2C\n\nSixth term: + (3/2) sin C\n\nCombine like terms:\n\nFor cos\u00b2 C: 3/4 cos\u00b2 C\n\nFor sin\u00b2 C: 1/4 sin\u00b2 C - sin\u00b2 C = -3/4 sin\u00b2 C\n\nFor sin C cos C: \u221a3/2 sin C cos C\n\nConstants: -3/4\n\nOthers: + (3/2) sin C\n\nSo the equation becomes:\n\n3/4 cos\u00b2 C - 3/4 sin\u00b2 C + \u221a3/2 sin C cos C + (3/2) sin C - 3/4 = 0\n\nFactor out 3/4 from the first two terms:\n\n3/4 (cos\u00b2 C - sin\u00b2 C) + \u221a3/2 sin C cos C + (3/2) sin C - 3/4 = 0\n\nNote that cos\u00b2 C - sin\u00b2 C = cos 2C\n\nSo:\n\n3/4 cos 2C + \u221a3/2 sin C cos C + (3/2) sin C - 3/4 = 0\n\nAlso, note that sin C cos C = (1/2) sin 2C, so \u221a3/2 sin C cos C = \u221a3/4 sin 2C\n\nThus:\n\n3/4 cos 2C + \u221a3/4 sin 2C + (3/2) sin C - 3/4 = 0\n\nHmm, this is getting complicated. Maybe there's a better approach. Let's think.\n\nAlternatively, since we have A + C = 120 degrees, maybe we can set angle C as x, then angle A is 120 - x, and try to express the equation in terms of x.\n\nLet me let angle C = x, so angle A = 120\u00b0 - x.\n\nThen, substituting into the equation sin\u00b2A = 3/4 + sin\u00b2x - (3/2) sin x.\n\nBut sin\u00b2A = sin\u00b2(120\u00b0 - x). Let's compute sin(120\u00b0 - x):\n\nsin(120\u00b0 - x) = sin 120\u00b0 cos x - cos 120\u00b0 sin x = (\u221a3/2) cos x - (-1/2) sin x = (\u221a3/2 cos x + 1/2 sin x)\n\nTherefore, sin\u00b2A = (\u221a3/2 cos x + 1/2 sin x)^2 = 3/4 cos\u00b2x + (\u221a3/2)(1/2) * 2 sin x cos x + 1/4 sin\u00b2x = 3/4 cos\u00b2x + \u221a3/2 sin x cos x + 1/4 sin\u00b2x.\n\nSo, sin\u00b2A = 3/4 cos\u00b2x + \u221a3/2 sin x cos x + 1/4 sin\u00b2x.\n\nSet equal to the right-hand side:\n\n3/4 + sin\u00b2x - (3/2) sin x.\n\nTherefore:\n\n3/4 cos\u00b2x + \u221a3/2 sin x cos x + 1/4 sin\u00b2x = 3/4 + sin\u00b2x - (3/2) sin x\n\nBring all terms to the left:\n\n3/4 cos\u00b2x + \u221a3/2 sin x cos x + 1/4 sin\u00b2x - 3/4 - sin\u00b2x + (3/2) sin x = 0\n\nCombine like terms:\n\n3/4 cos\u00b2x - 3/4 (since -3/4) + 1/4 sin\u00b2x - sin\u00b2x + \u221a3/2 sin x cos x + (3/2) sin x = 0\n\nSimplify:\n\n3/4 cos\u00b2x - 3/4 - 3/4 sin\u00b2x + \u221a3/2 sin x cos x + (3/2) sin x = 0\n\nFactor 3/4:\n\n3/4 (cos\u00b2x - 1 - sin\u00b2x) + \u221a3/2 sin x cos x + (3/2) sin x = 0\n\nBut cos\u00b2x - 1 - sin\u00b2x = cos\u00b2x - sin\u00b2x -1 = cos 2x -1\n\nWait, cos\u00b2x - sin\u00b2x = cos 2x, so cos\u00b2x -1 - sin\u00b2x = cos 2x -1.\n\nBut that might not help directly. Alternatively, note that cos\u00b2x - sin\u00b2x = cos 2x, so the expression is 3/4 (cos 2x - 1) + \u221a3/2 sin x cos x + (3/2) sin x = 0.\n\nSo:\n\n3/4 cos 2x - 3/4 + \u221a3/2 sin x cos x + (3/2) sin x = 0\n\nLet me write everything in terms of sin x and cos x, perhaps. Let's see:\n\nAlso, note that cos 2x = 1 - 2 sin\u00b2x. But maybe not helpful here.\n\nAlternatively, let's use substitution variables. Let\u2019s set t = sin x. Then cos x = \u221a(1 - t\u00b2), but that may complicate. Alternatively, factor terms:\n\nLooking at the equation:\n\n3/4 cos 2x + \u221a3/2 sin x cos x + (3/2) sin x - 3/4 = 0\n\nLet me try to combine terms:\n\nFirst term: 3/4 cos 2x\n\nSecond term: \u221a3/2 sin x cos x\n\nThird term: (3/2) sin x\n\nFourth term: -3/4\n\nHmm. Maybe factor out 3/4:\n\n3/4 (cos 2x - 1) + \u221a3/2 sin x cos x + (3/2) sin x = 0\n\nAlternatively, use double-angle identities:\n\nNote that sin x cos x = (1/2) sin 2x, so \u221a3/2 sin x cos x = \u221a3/4 sin 2x\n\nAnd cos 2x is as is.\n\nSo:\n\n3/4 cos 2x + \u221a3/4 sin 2x + (3/2) sin x - 3/4 = 0\n\nHmm, combining the first two terms:\n\n3/4 cos 2x + \u221a3/4 sin 2x can be written as (3/4) cos 2x + (\u221a3/4) sin 2x. Let's factor out 1/4:\n\n(1/4)(3 cos 2x + \u221a3 sin 2x) + (3/2) sin x - 3/4 = 0\