c to $C_1$ about this bisecting plane. Notice that $C_1'$ lies in the plane $PA_1B_1$, and:\n   \\[\n   \\triangle A_1B_1C_1' \\cong \\triangle A_1B_1C_1\n   \\]\n   Because $QC_1' = QC_1$, the line segment $P C_{1}^{\\prime}$ in plane $PA_1B_1$ satisfies:\n   \\[\n   P C_{1}^{\\prime} < P Q + Q C_{1}^{\\prime} = P Q + Q C = P C_{1} = d\n   \\]\n\n4. **Geometrical Construction on Plane:**\n   In plane $PA_1B_1$, draw a circle with center $P$ and radius $d$. Point $C_1'$ lies inside this circle. \n   Through $A_1$, draw $A_1A_0$ tangent to the circle at $A_0$, and through $B_1$, draw $B_1B_0$ tangent to the circle at $B_0$.\n   Clearly, the following inequalities hold:\n   \\[\n   \\angle PA_1C_1' < \\angle PA_1A_0 \\quad \\text{and} \\quad \\angle PB_1C_1' < \\angle PB_1B_0\n   \\]\n   In right-angled $\\triangle PA_1A_0$, we have:\n   \\[\n   \\angle PA_1A_0 = \\arcsin \\frac{d}{a} < \\frac{\\frac{\\pi}{2} - \\alpha}{4}\n   \\]\n   Similarly:\n   \\[\n   \\angle PB_1B_0 = \\arcsin \\frac{d}{a} < \\frac{\\frac{\\pi}{2} - \\alpha}{4}\n   \\]\n\n5. **Calculating Internal Angles:**\n   The internal angle $\\angle A_1C_1'B_1$ equals:\n   \\[\n   \\angle A_1C_1'B_1 = \\angle A_1PB_1 \\pm \\angle PA_1C_1' \\pm \\angle PB_1C_1'\n   \\]\n   Thus:\n   \\[\n   \\angle A_1C_1'B_1 < \\angle A_1PB_1 + \\angle PA_1A_0 + \\angle PB_1B_0\n   \\]\n   Substituting known values:\n   \\[\n   \\angle A_1C_1'B_1 < \\alpha + \\frac{\\frac{\\pi}{2} - \\alpha}{4} + \\frac{\\frac{\\pi}{2} - \\alpha}{4} = \\alpha + \\frac{\\frac{\\pi}{2} - \\alpha}{2} = \\alpha + \\frac{\\pi}{4} - \\frac{\\alpha}{2}\n   \\]\n   Simplifying:\n   \\[\n   \\alpha + \\frac{\\pi}{4} - \\frac{\\alpha}{2} < \\frac{\\pi}{2}\n   \\]\n\n6. **Acute Angles Verification:**\n   For the angle at $A_1$, $\\angle C_1'A_1B_1 \\leq \\angle PA_1B_1 + \\angle PA_1C_1'$.\n   As $\\angle PA_1B_1 = \\frac{\\pi}{2} - \\frac{\\alpha}{2}$ and $\\angle PA_1C_1' < \\angle PA_1A_0 = \\arcsin \\frac{d}{a} < \\frac{\\alpha}{4}$:\n   \\[\n  