ega\\) because the homothety mapping \\(\\omega_1\\) to \\(\\omega_2\\) preserves the tangency conditions imposed by \\(\\omega\\)'s position and radius.\n\n**Final Answer**: The common external tangents of \\(\\omega_1\\) and \\(\\omega_2\\) intersect on \\(\\omega\\), as required. \\(\\boxed{\\text{The intersection point lies on } \\omega}\\)", "ground_truth_solution": "\nThe problem involves a convex quadrilateral \\(ABCD\\) and two incircles of triangles \\(ABC\\) and \\(ADC\\). We aim to prove that the common external tangents of these two incircles intersect on a given circle \\(\\omega\\).\n\nTo tackle the problem, we apply two lemmas. A detailed breakdown of the arguments follows below.\n\n**Lemma 1:**\n\nGiven a convex quadrilateral \\(ABCD\\) with a circle inscribed in \\(\\angle ABC\\) and tangent to extensions of lines \\(AD\\) and \\(CD\\), we have:\n\\[\nAB + AD = CB + CD.\n\\]\n\n**Proof of Lemma 1:**\n\nConsider the circle \\(\\omega\\) inscribed in \\(\\angle ABC\\) and tangent to the lines \\(AB, BC, CD, DA\\) at points \\(K, L, M, N\\) respectively. We observe:\n\\[\nAB + AD = (BK - AK) + (AN - DN),\n\\]\nand\n\\[\nCB + CD = (BL - CL) + (CM - DM).\n\\]\n\nAs the circle's tangents from a point are equal, we have:\n\\[\nBK = BL, \\quad DN = DM, \\quad AK = AN, \\quad CL = CM.\n\\]\n\nInserting these equal tangents into our equations results in:\n\\[\nAB + AD = (BL - AK) + (AN - DM) = CB + CD,\n\\]\nproving the lemma.\n\n**Lemma 2:**\n\nThe incircle of \\(\\triangle ABC\\) touches side \\(AC\\) at \\(P\\). Let \\(PP'\\) be a diameter of the incircle passing through \\(P\\), and let line \\(BP'\\) intersect \\(AC\\) at \\(Q\\). Then, \\(Q\\) is the tangency point of side \\(AC\\) with the excircle opposite \\(B\\).\n\n**Proof of Lemma 2:**\n\nConsider the triangle \\(\\triangle A'BC'\\) where \\(A'\\) and \\(C'\\) are points where incircle \\(\\omega_1\\) meets \\(BA\\) and \\(BC\\) respectively.\n\nWe know:\n\\[\n\\omega_1 \\text{ is the excircle relative to } A'C'.\n\\]\n\nSince \\(A'C' \\parallel AC\\), the homothety centered at \\(B\\) with ratio \\(\\frac{BQ}{BP'}\\) maps \\(\\omega_1\\) to the excircle of \\(\\triangle ABC\\). As the homothety maps \\(P'\\) to \\(Q\\), the point \\(Q\\) is the tangency point with the excircle.\n\nNow, we transition to the main proof.\n\n### Proof:\n\n1. The incircles of \\(\\triangle ABC\\) and \\(\\triangle ADC\\), \\(\\omega_1\\) and \\(\\omega_2\\), touch \\(AC\\) at points \\(P\\) and \\(Q\\) respectively. \n\n2. From Lemma 1, applying the property:\n   \\[\n   AB - BC = CD - AD,\n   \\]\n   we derive the relation \\(AP = CQ\\) for the distances from \\(A\\) and \\(C\\) to the respective tangency points.\n\n3. Using Lemma 2, note that \\(P\\) and \\(Q\\) are points such that:\n   \\[\n   A P = (AC + AB - BC) / 2, \\quad C Q = (CA + CD - AD) / 2.\n   \\]\n\n4. Since \\(AP = CQ\\), and recalling that \\(P \\neq Q\\) (as \\(AB \\neq BC\\)), we confirm:\n\n   The diameters \\(PP'\\) and \\(QQ'\\) of \\(\\omega_1\\) and \\(\\omega_2\\) are perpendicular to \\(AC\\). \n\n5. The points \\(B, P'\\) and \\(Q\\) are collinear, as the homothety centered at \\(B\\) with the ratio \\(\\frac{BT}{BP'}\\) maps \\(\\omega_1\\) to \\(\\omega\\). Similarly, \\(D, Q'\\) and \\(T\\) co-align as homothety with the ratio \\(\\frac{DT}{DQ'}\\) maps \\(\\omega_2\\) to \\(\\omega\\).\n\n6. By construction, \\(T\\) is the center of a homothety with a positive ratio that transforms \\(\\omega_1\\) to \\(\\omega_2\\). Considering the common external tangents intersect at this point \\(T\\), we conclude:\n\n\\[\n\\boxed{\\text{The common external tangents of } \\omega_1 \\text{ and } \\omega_2 \\text{ intersect on } \\omega.}\n\\]\n", "domain": "math", "source": "numina_math", "test_cases": null, "starter_code": null}, {"problem": "The sum of five non-negative numbers is equal to one.\n\nProve that they can be arranged in a circle such that the sum of all five pairwise products of neighboring numbe