allelogram $ACDE$ is the same as the area of $ACHK$ because $HK \\parallel AC$ and both have the same base $AC$ and equal heights relative to $AC$.\n     \\[\n     S_{ACDE} = S_{ACHK}\n     \\]\n   - The area of parallelogram $ACHK$ is the same as the area of parallelogram $ATNL$ because $HK \\parallel TN$ and both have the same base $AT$ and heights relative to the line parallel to $AC$.\n     \\[\n     S_{ACHK} = S_{ATNL}\n     \\]\n   - Thus, we can write\n     \\[\n     S_{ACDE} = S_{ATNL}\n     \\]\n\n4. **Considering the Second Parallelogram**:\n   - Similarly, the area of parallelogram $BCFG$ is the same as the area of $BCHP$ because $HP \\parallel BC$.\n     \\[\n     S_{BCFG} = S_{BCHP}\n     \\]\n   - The area of $BCHP$ is the same as the area of $BMNT$ for similar reasons mentioned in step 3.\n     \\[\n     S_{BCHP} = S_{BMNT}\n     \\]\n   - Thus,\n     \\[\n     S_{BCFG} = S_{BMNT}\n     \\]\n\n5. **Combining Results**:\n   - Adding the equivalent areas derived:\n     \\[\n     S_{ACDE} + S_{BCFG} = S_{ATNL} + S_{BMNT}\n     \\]\n\n6. **Relating to the Parallelogram $ABML$**:\n   - The sum of the areas of $ATNL$ and $BMNT$ is the area of the parallelogram $ABML$ because $T$ and $N$ lie on line $HT$ parallel to $LM$, completing parallelogram $ABML$:\n     \\[\n     S_{ABML} = S_{ATNL} + S_{BMNT}\n     \\]\n\n7. **Conclusion**:\n   - By combining the equalities and the final expression, we conclude:\n     \\[\n     S_{ABML} = S_{ACDE} + S_{BCFG}\n     \\]\n\nThus, we have shown that the area of parallelogram $ABML$ is indeed equal to the sum of the areas of parallelograms $ACDE$ and $BCFG$.\n\n\\[\n\\boxed{S_{ABML} = S_{ACDE} + S_{BCFG}}\n\\]", "domain": "math", "source": "numina_math", "test_cases": null, "starter_code": null}, {"problem": "Let \\( ABC \\) be a triangle, and let the bisector of \\( \\angle BAC \\) meet \\( BC \\) at \\( D \\). Let \\( O, O_1, O_2 \\) be the circumcenters of triangles \\( ABC, ABD \\), and \\( ADC \\), respectively. Prov