2q ) )\n\nSimilarly, find O2, the circumcenter of ADC. Points A(0,0), C(p,-q), D(d,0).\n\nThe perpendicular bisector of AC: midpoint is (p/2, -q/2 ), slope of AC is (-q)/p, so perpendicular bisector slope is p/q.\n\nEquation: y + q/2 = (p/q)(x - p/2 )\n\nPerpendicular bisector of AD: same as before, x = d/2.\n\nTherefore, O2 is the intersection of x = d/2 and the perpendicular bisector of AC.\n\nSubstitute x = d/2 into the equation:\n\ny + q/2 = (p/q)(d/2 - p/2 ) = (p/q)( (d - p)/2 )\n\nTherefore,\n\ny = (p(d - p))/(2q ) - q/2 = [ p(d - p) - q\u00b2 ] / (2q )\n\nTherefore, coordinates of O2 are ( d/2, [ p(d - p) - q\u00b2 ] / (2q ) )\n\nNow, compute OO1 and OO2.\n\nCoordinates of O: ( (p\u00b2 + q\u00b2)/(2p ), 0 )\n\nCoordinates of O1: ( d/2, [ q\u00b2 - p(d - p) ] / (2q ) )\n\nCoordinates of O2: ( d/2, [ p(d - p) - q\u00b2 ] / (2q ) )\n\nCompute distance OO1:\n\nFirst, compute the x-coordinate difference: (d/2 ) - ( (p\u00b2 + q\u00b2)/(2p ) ) = [ dp - p\u00b2 - q\u00b2 ] / (2p )\n\nY-coordinate difference: [ q\u00b2 - p(d - p) ] / (2q ) - 0 = [ q\u00b2 - pd + p\u00b2 ] / (2q )\n\nTherefore, OO1\u00b2 = [ (dp - p\u00b2 - q\u00b2 )\u00b2 / (4p\u00b2 ) ] + [ (q\u00b2 - pd + p\u00b2 )\u00b2 / (4q\u00b2 ) ]\n\nSimilarly, compute distance OO2:\n\nX-coordinate difference is same as above: [ dp - p\u00b2 - q\u00b2 ] / (2p )\n\nY-coordinate difference: [ p(d - p) - q\u00b2 ] / (2q ) - 0 = [ pd - p\u00b2 - q\u00b2 ] / (2q )\n\nTherefore, OO2\u00b2 = [ (dp - p\u00b2 - q\u00b2 )\u00b2 / (4p\u00b2 ) ] + [ (pd - p\u00b2 - q\u00b2 )\u00b2 / (4q\u00b2 ) ]\n\nNotice that the x-coordinate differences are the same for OO1 and OO2, and the y-coordinate differences for OO1 is [ q\u00b2 - pd + p\u00b2 ] / (2q ) and for OO2 is [ pd - p\u00b2 - q\u00b2 ] / (2q ). These y-coordinate terms are negatives of each other:\n\n[ q\u00b2 - pd + p\u00b2 ] = - [ pd - p\u00b2 - q\u00b2 ]\n\nTherefore, when squared, they become the same value. Therefore, OO1\u00b2 = OO2\u00b2, so OO1 = OO2.\n\nTherefore, the distances are equal.\n\nHence proved.", "deepseek_solution": "To prove that \\( OO_1 = OO_2 \\), where \\( O, O_1, O_2 \\) are the circumcenters of triangles \\( ABC, ABD, \\) and \\( ADC \\) respectively, and \\( D \\) is the point where the angle bisector of \\( \\angle BAC \\) meets \\( BC \\), we can utilize coordinate geometry and symmetry.\n\n**Step-by-Step Explanation:**\n\n1. **Coordinate Setup:**\n   - Place \\( A \\) at the origin \\((0,0)\\), \\( B \\) at \\((p, q)\\), and \\( C \\) at \\((p, -q)\\) to ensure symmetry across the x-axis (which is the angle bisector \\( AD \\)).\n   - Let \\( D \\) lie on \\( BC \\). By the Angle Bisector Theorem and symmetry, \\( D \\) is at \\((d, 0)\\).\n\n2. **Circumcenter of \\( ABC \\):**\n   - The circumcenter \\( O \\) of \\( \\triangle ABC \\) lies at the intersection of the perpendicular bisectors of \\( AB \\) and \\( AC \\).\n   - Solving these, \\( O \\) is found to be \\(\\left( \\frac{p^2 + q^2}{2p}, 0 \\right)\\), lying on the x-axis (along \\( AD \\)).\n\n3. **Circumcenters of \\( ABD \\) and \\( ADC \\):**\n   - **For \\( \\triangle ABD \\):**\n     - The perpendicular bisectors of \\( AB \\) and \\( AD \\) intersect at \\( O_1 \\left( \\frac{d}{2}, \\frac{q^2 - p(d - p)}{2q} \\right) \\).\n   - **For \\( \\triangle ADC \\):**\n     - Similarly, the perpendicular bisectors of \\( AC \\) and \\( AD \\) intersect at \\( O_2 \\left( \\frac{d}{2}, \\frac{p(d - p) - q^2}{2q} \\right) \\).\n\n4. **Distance Calculation:**\n   - Compute distances \\( OO_1 \\) and \\( OO_2 \\):\n     - **X-coordinate difference:** Same for both, \\(\\frac{dp - p^2 - q^2}{2p}\\).\n     - **Y-coordinate differences:** For \\( OO_1 \\), \\(\\frac{q^2 - pd + p^2}{2q}\\); for \\( OO_2 \\), \\(\\frac{pd - p^2 - q^2}{2q}\\) (which are negatives of each other).\n   - Squaring these differences shows \\( OO_1^2 = OO_2^2 \\), hence \\( OO_1 = OO_2 \\).\n\n**Conclusion:**  \nBy exploiting the symmetry induced by the angle bisector \\( AD \\) and calculating coordinates, we demonstrated that \\( OO_1 \\) and \\( OO_2 \\) are equal in length. Thus, the circumcenters \\( O_1 \\) and \\( O_2 \\) are equidistant from \\( O \\).\n\n\\(\\boxed{OO_1 = OO_2}\\)", "ground_truth_solution": "Given the triangle \\(ABC\\) with bisector \\(AD\\) of \\(\\angle BAC\\), we need to prove that \\(OO_1 = OO_2\\), where \\(O\\), \\(O_1\\), and \\(O_2\\) are the circumcenters of triangles \\(ABC\\), \\(ABD\\), and \\(ACD\\), respectively.\n\n1. **Identify Perpendicular Bisectors:**\n    - The circumcenter of a triangle is found at the intersection of the perpendicular bisectors of the triangle's sides.\n    - Let \\(P\\) be the intersection of the perpendicular bisectors of \\(AB\\), \\(BC\\), and \\(CA\\). Then \\(O\\), the circumcenter of \\(\\triangle ABC\\), is located at \\(P\\).\n\n2. **Circumcenters of \\(\\triangle ABD\\) and \\(\\triangle ACD\\):**\n    - Let \\(P_1\\) be the intersection of the perpendicular bisectors of \\(AB\\), \\(AD\\), and \\(BD\\). Then \\(O_1\\), the circumcenter of \\(\\triangle ABD\\), is located at \\(P_1\\).\n    - Let \\(P_2\\) be the intersection of the perpendicular bisectors of \\(AC\\), \\(AD\\), and \\(CD\\). Then \\(O_2\\), the circumcenter of \\(\\triangle ACD\\), is located at \\(P_2\\).\n\n3. **Perpendicular Relationships:**\n    - Note that \\(OO_1 \\perp AB\\) because both \\(O\\) and \\(O_1\\) lie on the perpendicular bisector of side \\(AB\\).\n    - Similarly, \\(OO_2 \\perp AC\\) as both \\(O\\) and \\(O_2\\) lie on the perpendicular bisector of side \\(AC\\).\n    - And, \\(O_1O_2 \\perp AD\\) since both \\(O_1\\) and \\(O_2\\) form the perpendicular bisectors involving point \\(D\\).\n\n4. **Angles between Perpendicular Lines:**\n    - The (counterclockwise) angle of rotation between lines \\(OO_1\\) and \\(O_1O_2\\) is the same between the perpendicular lines \\(AB\\) and \\(AD\\).\n    - Hence, \\(\\angle OOO_1O_2\\) is equivalent to \\(\\angle BAD\\).\n    - Similarly, \\(\\angle OO_1O_2O\\) corresponds to \\(\\angle DAC\\). \n\n5. **Equality of Angles:**\n    - We are given that \\(\\angle BAD = \\angle DAC\\). Therefore, \\(\\angle OOO_1O_2 = \\angle OO_1O_2O\\).\n    - This ensures that \\(\\triangle OOO_1O_2\\) is an isosceles triangle.\n\n6. **Conclusion:**\n    - Because \\(\\triangle OOO_1O_2\\) is isosceles and the angles at \\(O_1\\) are equal,\n      \\[\n      OO_1 = OO_2\n      \\]\n\nThus, we have proven that \\(OO_1 = OO_2\\), confirming the triangle \\(\\triangle OOO_1O_2\\) is isosceles.\n\n\\[\n\\boxed{OO_1 = OO_2}\n\\]", "domain": "math", "source": "numina_math", "test_cases": null, "starter_code": null}, {"problem": "Let \\(\\left\\{\\xi_{n}\\right\\}_{n \\geqslant 1}\\) be a sequence of random variables. Show that the set \\(\\left\\{\\xi_{n} \\rightarrow\\right\\}\\), i.e., the set of \\(\\omega \\in \\Omega\\) where \\(\\xi_{n}(\\omega)\\) converges, can be represented as follows:\n\n\\[\n\\left\\{\\xi_{n} \\rightarrow\\right\\}=\\bigcap_{n=1}^{\\infty} \\bigcup_{m=1}^{\\infty} \\bigcap_{k=m}^{\\infty}\\left\\{\\sup _{l \\geqslant k}\\left|\\xi_{l}-\\xi_{k}\\right| \\leqslant n^{-1}\\right\\}\n\\]\n\nSimilarly, the set \\(\\left\\{\\xi_{n} \\nrightarrow\\right\\}\\), where \\(\\xi_{n}\\) diverges, can be represented as follows:\n\n\\[\n\\left\\{\\xi_{n} \\nrightarrow\\right\\}=\\bigcup_{n=1}^{\\infty} \\bigcap_{m=1}^{\\infty} \\bigcup_{k=m}^{\\infty}\\left\\{\\sup _{l \\geqslant k}\\left|\\xi_{l}-\\xi_{k}\\right|>n^{-1}\\right\\}\n\\]", "deepseek_reasoning": "Okay, so I need to show that the set where the sequence of random variables \u03be\u2099 converges can be written as that big intersection and union thing. Let me start by recalling what it means for a sequence of real numbers to converge. A sequence a\u2099 converges if and only if it's a Cauchy sequence. That is, for every \u03b5 > 0, there exists an integer m such that for all k, l \u2265 m, |a\u2096 - a\u2097| < \u03b5. But here, the repr